\par\par \b \uc2 Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  foray last Friday seen near Y1 trln.  \b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 11:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 8 (Reuters) - Japan probably sold nearly one trillion yen ($9 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, when the yen fell to five-month lows against the dollar, money market analysts estimated on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's money market projections for Tuesday, the day Friday's transactions settle, showed fund transfers from the public sector to the private sector at 1.14 trillion yen, about 700-900 billion yen more than analysts' original projections.  \par \par \uc2 The discrepancy suggested that Tokyo has been continuing heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the yen's six percent retreat from 3-= year highs set last month, confounding expectations that it would end its controversial market foray once the yen's relentless rise had stopped.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no reason why the fund transfer component should show such a big discrepancy on March 9. It is most likely because of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities that is said to have taken place on March 5," said an analyst at a financial institution who asked not to be named.  \par \par \uc2 The fund transfer component of the money market projections includes such flows as tax payments and salaries paid to public servants. The overall projections include banknote issuance and government debt issuance and redemptions.  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell to five-month lows near 112.30 yen to the dollar on Friday, despite weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data that sent the dollar down against the euro. Dealers cited \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the reason.  \par \par \uc2 Late last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between January 29 and February 25 totalled 3.34 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 That followed a monthly record of 7.15 trillion yen in January - bringing the total for just the first two months of this year to about half of last year's record 20 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has been worried that a runaway rise in the yen would derail its export-led economic recovery and exacerbate protracted price deflation that has subdued domestic investment and spending.  \par \par \uc2 While its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has drawn criticism from its trade partners, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has insisted that its battle to end the yen's rise has been vindicated by a statement issued last month by the Group of Seven industrial countries that said excess volatility in exchange rates was undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040308e038001l0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
