\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Dollar's fate hazy despite Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have succeeded for now in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the dollar up seven percent on the yen since early February, and analysts expect more yen-selling by Tokyo at least until the end of the month.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts see the dollar coming under pressure given receding prospects for a near-term U.S. interest rate rise due to a struggling jobs market, as well as technical constraints on Japan's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspect Japanese authorities of acting in the market even after last week's criticism by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be aiming to push the dollar back up towards 115 yen, where it stood before a September meeting of Group of Seven industrial countries held in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "It's possible they will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month until the dollar rises to around 115 yen," said Takashi Yamanaka, an economist at UFJ Bank.  \par \par \uc2 A call by the G7 at the Dubai meeting for more flexibility in exchange rates accelerated a two-year decline in the dollar, which sank to around 105.15 yen on February 11, its lowest in three and a half years.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the dollar rose more than one yen to a five-month high of 112.30 yen despite falling against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on surprisingly soft U.S. jobs data. Traders think the rise was due to large scale-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Wariness over more such Japanese action remained high on Monday, supporting the dollar at around 112.15 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials worry a high yen could hurt exports, the engine of the economy's recovery from a decade of stagnation. But with economic growth in the last quarter of 2003 a 13-year high, a growing number of analysts see \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's justification weakening.  \par \par \uc2 UNABLE TO SUSTAIN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 And although the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) is seen profiting due to the recent rally from its dollar purchases made around the time of the February low, many analysts believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in any case unsustainable.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting for the MOF, sold about 10.5 trillion yen ($93.61 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the first two months of this year - more than half the annual record of 20 trillion yen in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 To maintain the current level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the MOF makes an enormous issuance of five trillion yen of short-term financing bills (FBs) per week.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not convinced (the MOF) is going to continue doing this for several months coming. I think this is a trend that's particularly inherent ahead of the financial year-end. I don't think they can keep it up forever," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts think the MOF will continue to support the dollar until the end of the month to help Japanese companies dress up their books for the financial year-end on March 31, but will gradually loosen their grip from April.  \par \par \uc2 "Once April comes, they will turn cautious about aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the G7 meeting coming up," said UFJ's Yamanaka, referring to the April 24 meeting in Washington of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors.  \par \par \uc2 SHORT-TERM SUCCESS  \par \par \uc2 Soichi Okuda, a senior economist at Sumitomo Corp, said that in the short run Japanese authorities may have succeeded in reversing the dollar's downtrend, given a huge shift in speculators' positions.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission said speculators in yen futures built fresh net short positions in the week that ended on February 24 for the first time since early August.  \par \par \uc2 During the week that ended on March 2, speculators extended their net short positions to 22,312 contracts from 12,181 contracts in the previous week.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, speculators have waved a white flag against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said, adding that a growing number of players expect a rise to around 114 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "But fundamentally, the dollar's downside risk remains, especially after a weak U.S. jobs report raised speculation that the Fed will not raise rates this year."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department reported only 21,000 jobs were created last month, well short of market expectations for an extra 125,000 jobs. ($1=112.16 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040308e038000zx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
