\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Plunges Vs Yen; BOJ May Have Pulled Bid - Traders  \b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar plunged sharply against the yen in early afternoon New York trade Monday, triggering huge stop-loss orders after Japanese monetary authorities apparently pulled their bid for dollars, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "They took out their official bids and stops are being triggered," said a trader in New York.  \par \par \uc2 A second trader in New York said the stop-loss sell orders below Y112.00 were huge, worth hundreds of millions of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "These guys aren't stupid," said the second New York trader, referring to the Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "They would have heard about all the stops lying below Y112.00 and it looks like they've just let the market clear itself out. I wouldn't be surprised to see them back in" buying dollars at these lower levels, he added.  \par \par \uc2 A short while ago Monday, the dollar plunged almost a full yen to an intraday low of around Y111.20 from around Y112.15. Official bids had been lying in the Y112.00-Y112.10 area all day Monday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's plunge against the yen also rippled through other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs. The euro spiked up suddenly to over $1.2400 from around $1.2350.  \par \par \uc2 The apparent withdrawal of official Japanese bids for dollars came not long after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated his view that the global financial system is more stable when \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are left to flexible, competitive markets.  \par \par \uc2 "We favor a strong dollar, but the relative value of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are best set through open and competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Snow said following a speech to the National Association of State Treasurers.  \par \par \uc2 Snow said allowing market forces to work unfettered allows \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values to send proper price signals that encourage optimal investment decisions.  \par \par \uc2 "Market forces help alleviate internal pressures that can build up," Snow said, adding that allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adjust can also help the global economy work through imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets selling yen for dollars, traders said. It's estimated the MoF bought as much as $20 billion - thought to be a record for one day - trying to keep the yen down amid a global selloff in the dollar following the weak U.S. employment report.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 03-08-04 1411ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040308e0380002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
