\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls on shabby U.S. payrolls report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 15:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment; changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply on Friday after a weak U.S. payrolls report for February showed the economy added far fewer jobs than expected, diminishing chances the Federal Reserve will raise U.S. interest rates in 2004, a factor behind recent dollar strength.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, however, was little changed against the yen after the report as investors remain wary of Japan's concerted effort to buy dollars for yen in an effort to help exports.  \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported only 21,000 new jobs last month, compared with an increase of 97,000 jobs in January, itself a downward revision. The jobless rate meanwhile held steady at 5.6 percent in February.  \par \par \uc2 Economists forecasted the addition of 125,000 jobs to the nation's payrolls and the jobless rate to remain at 5.6 percent. Many investors had been looking for even stronger data and bought the dollar earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 "It puts markets in position of wait yet another month for the promised payrolls gains to materialize," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, referring to predictions by Federal Reserve officials that the labor market would improve.  \par \par \uc2 "I think this would reinforce the Fed holding rates steady for a very long time," she said.  \par \par \uc2 The weak payrolls number shows that the job market continues to improve but lags other parts of the U.S. economy that have been showing steady signs of improvement in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 Bets for a strong jobs report boosted the dollar this week to 2004 peaks versus the yen and the euro. A stronger gain might have freed up the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates from the current 1 percent, the lowest since 1958, sooner rather than later. A rate hike would make yields on U.S. assets more attractive to investors, thereby increasing demand for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 In early New York trade, the euro surged 1.48 percent to a session high $1.2384 , according to Reuters data. The dollar held at 111.01 yen , nearly unchanged on the day. The euro traded at 137.55 yen, a gain of more 1.40 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 1.42 percent to 1.2748 Swiss francs, while sterling climbed to $1.8443 , a gain of 1.16 percent on the day. The Australian dollar shot up 1.32 percent to US$0.7602 against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 After the dollar rallied nearly five cents earlier this week, slight support for the euro emerged on Thursday from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which held rates steady at 2 percent and gave no sign an interest rate cut was near.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  received help earlier climbing above 111.40 yen , its highest since November 3, on speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen after stopping its rise at 3-1/2 year highs versus the U.S. dollar set last month.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently in dollar/yen from around 110.50 on Thursday, raising speculating Japan is determined to prevent the dollar's recovery from faltering.  \par \par \uc2 "No one is ready to put on short dollar/yen positions," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in Paris.  \par \par \uc2 Also in focus is a news conference with ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet and Bank of France Governor Christian Noyer at 9:30 a.m. EST (1430 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Trichet said rates were appropriate and the economic risks balanced - ECB code for unchanged monetary policy in the months ahead.  \par \par \uc2 This disappointed those in the markets who thought the recent high levels of the euro - which hit a record peak of $1.2927 in February - might lead the ECB to ease rates to help euro zone exporters.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Burton Frierson).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040306e035003vs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
