\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops on weak jobs report, Fed outlook.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply on Friday against most major rivals after payrolls data showed the U.S. economy added far fewer jobs than expected in February, diminishing chances for a U.S. interest rate hike in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Market expectations of an increase in official interest rates, which would make yields on U.S. assets more attractive, had been a factor behind recent dollar strength.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback weakened about 1.8 percent against the euro and nearly 2 percent against the Swiss franc. The U.S. dollar also posted losses of more than 1 percent against sterling, the Canadian dollar and the Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that as the end of the day in London comes, we could see some more weakness in the dollar versus the euro," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America Securities in New York. "I don't think there's an upward bounce at this point. We do not think that the dollar decline is over."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department reported only 21,000 new jobs were created last month, compared with an increase of 97,000 jobs in January, itself a downward revision. The jobless rate held steady at 5.6 percent in February.  \par \par \uc2 Economists had forecast the addition of 125,000 jobs and for the jobless rate to remain at 5.6 percent. Many investors had been looking for even stronger job creation and bought the dollar earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 "It puts markets in the position of (having to) wait yet another month for the promised payrolls gains to materialize," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, referring to predictions by Federal Reserve officials that the labor market would improve.  \par \par \uc2 "I think this would reinforce the Fed holding rates steady for a very long time," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets pushed back their expectations for an interest rate hike until after the November U.S. presidential election, Wall Street economists said.  \par \par \uc2 The weak payrolls number shows that the job market still lags other parts of the U.S. economy that have been showing steady signs of improvement in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 Bets for a strong jobs report boosted the dollar this week to 2004 peaks versus the yen and the euro. A stronger gain might have freed up the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates from the current 1 percent, the lowest since 1958, sooner rather than later. A rate hike would make yields on U.S. assets more attractive to investors, increasing demand for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, however, was little changed against the yen after the report as investors remain wary of Japan's concerted effort to buy dollars for yen in an effort to help exports.  \par \par \uc2 In mid-morning New York trade, the euro was at $1.2410 . The dollar bought 111.00 yen , nearly unchanged on the day. The euro traded at 137.77 yen , a gain of 1.75 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to 1.2722 Swiss francs , while sterling climbed to $1.8472 , a gain of 1.27 percent on the day. The Australian dollar shot up 1.57 percent to US$0.7620 against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 After the dollar rallied nearly five cents against the euro this week, slight support for the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  emerged on Thursday from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which held rates steady at 2 percent and gave no sign an interest rate cut was near.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  received help earlier, climbing above 111.40 yen, its highest since Nov. 3, on speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen after stopping its rise at 3-1/2-year highs versus the dollar set last month.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently in dollar/yen from around 110.50 on Thursday, raising speculation Japan is determined to prevent the dollar's recovery from faltering.  \par \par \uc2 "No one is ready to put on short dollar/yen positions," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in Paris. (Additional reporting by Burton Frierson).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040305e035002pg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
