\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-Dollar hits 4-mth high vs yen ahead of key US data.   \b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In TOKYO story headlined "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Dollar hits 4-mth high vs yen\par  ahead of key US data" please read in third paragraph ....around\par  110.50 on Thursday.... instead of ....on Tuesday (correcting date\par  of suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ).\par      A corrected story follows.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Kazunori Takada   \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its month-long rally on Friday, hitting a four-month high versus the yen on hopes for strong U.S. jobs data later in the day and on growing speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to a high of 111.40 yen up from around 111.05 yen late in New York on Thursday, probing fresh four-month peaks for the fourth consecutive day. It is now nearly six percent above 3-= year lows set last month.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittenly from around 110.50 on Thursday, raising concerns that Japan might be determined to prevent the dollar's recovery from faltering.   \par \par \uc2 "When the dollar was struggling at lower levels, people blindly sold it on the assumption that the Japanese authorities would not continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the dollar bounced a little. But people aren't so sure anymore," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 At 0308 GMT, the dollar was at 111.37/45 yen.   \par \par \uc2 The euro was stuck around late U.S. levels at $1.2198/03 after seesaw trading on Thursday when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, as widely expected.   \par \par \uc2 CRUCIAL JOBS REPORT   \par \par \uc2 Traders said U.S. payroll figures, due at 1330 GMT, would likely be a key factor for whether the dollar continues its bull run and tests next resistance around 112 yen and a three-month peak of $1.2055 versus the euro.   \par \par \uc2 "The jobs data will be crucial for the dollar," said Kenji Kobayashi, senior manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.   \par \par \uc2 He said the dollar would likely continue moving upward if non-farm payrolls came in above 200,000 but if the figure turns out lower than 100,000, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come under "very strong" selling pressure.   \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a rise of 125,000 in February non-farm payrolls.   \par \par \uc2 Given that the stubbornly soft jobs market has been one reason why the Federal Reserve has yet to signal any imminent interest rate increases, a strong reading could push bond yields and other market interest rates significantly higher, supporting the dollar, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Although some players think the dollar could head back down if the jobs data disappoints, dollar bulls think any losses versus the yen will be limited due to wariness over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "As long as there is this nagging concern about the authorities stepping in again, it looks like even a drop below 111 yen is unlikely for now," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank.   \par \par \uc2 HOW MUCH MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data on Friday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's external reserves bulging to $776.86 billion at the end of February, a record high for the sixth straight month and up $35.61 billion from the end of January.   \par \par \uc2 The figures were in line with the MOF's announcement a week earlier that its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month had totalled 3.34 trillion yen ($30.07 billion). That brought the total for the first two months of this year above 10 trillion yen - already half of last year's record 20 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2 Doubts about how far \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were highlighted by comments earlier this week by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2 He said that an improving Japanese economy meant the large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would soon no longer meet \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy needs.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki defended Japan's stance, telling reporters on Friday that balancing stability and flexibility was important and that he saw no difference between Greenspan's views on Tokyo's policy and Japan's own views. ($1=111.08 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040305e035000bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
