\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar extends rally but Asian stocks fall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Hansford  \par \uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to four-month highs against the yen on Friday, as optimism grew ahead of U.S. jobs data, helping boost Japanese exporters while other Asian share markets fell in choppy trade.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit 111.43 yen, extending a month-long rally as investors acted ahead of February jobs data due later in the day, and as wariness persisted about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady against the dollar at around $1.22, but U.S. crude oil extended its gains to $36.90 as traders heeded signs the Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries would curb output next month.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Europe, financial bookmakers predicted London's FTSE 100 index\par would open up to five points lower, and Paris's CAC 40         was seen\par one point down at the open.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei average ended up 1.19 percent at 11,537.29, its highest close since June 6, 2002, as buying of shares in exporters on the weaker yen offset any downside after Intel (INTC.O) narrowed its revenue forecast in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, MSCI's broadest index of shares outside Japan was struggling to hold onto a 0.1 percent gain, with Taiwan shares falling from 41-month highs, and South Korean and Hong Kong counters also seeing red.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Tokyo market is just like a speeding bullet train now and nothing can stop its run, even Intel," said Masayoshi Okamoto, head of dealing at Jujiya Securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei has risen in seven of the past eight sessions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor Co Ltd (7267.T), which generates more than three-quarters of its sales abroad, rose 3.89 percent on hopes a firmer dollar would boost overseas sales. Bigger rival Toyota Motor Corp (7203.T) rose 2.35 percent to 3,920 yen, while Nissan Motor Co (7201.T) added 2.42 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors again bought into retail stocks, with Ito-Yokado Co Ltd (8264.T), the country's biggest retailer, up 2.12 percent on growing optimism over the nation's economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DOLLAR'S DAY  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is nearly six percent above 3-1/2-year lows set last month against the yen and traders said its ability to hold above 110 yen was making the market cautious that Japan might be determined to reinforce the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the latest catalyst for the dollar's rally has been rising expectations over Friday's non-farm payrolls data for February, which is due out at 1330 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a rise of 125,000 in U.S. non-farm payrolls and, given that the stubbornly soft jobs market has been one reason why the Federal Reserve has yet to signal any imminent interest rate increases, a strong reading could push bond yields and other market interest rates significantly higher, supporting the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said the figures would likely be a key factor for whether the dollar continues its bull run and tests its next resistance around 112 yen and a three-month peak of $1.2055 versus the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was steady against the dollar on Friday after seesaw trading the previous day, when the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged, as widely expected.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ASIAN STOCKS FALL  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere in Asia, the benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index was off Thursday's 22-month high, falling 0.23 percent to 905.38 points, while Taiwan shares came off a 3-= year high from early trade as investors reacted to Intel's disappointing forecast.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The TAIEX  share index ended down 1.29 percent at 6,943.68, with Nanya\par Tech Corp (2408.TW), Taiwan's largest memory chipmaker, losing 4.46\par percent and the wider tech sub-index         dropping 0.52 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index edged lower by 0.06 percent in afternoon trade, as wary investors dumped banking shares and kept an eye on whether funds were moving out of local equities on gains in the U.S. dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings Plc (HSBA.L) (0005.HK), the world's second-largest bank by market value, dropped 0.82 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index  rose 1.07 percent, while the Dow\par Jones industrial average        fell 0.05 percent on Thursday. Intel\par gave its forecast after the closing bell.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. crude oil jumped 26 cents to $36.90 per barrel, as investors also expressed concern over political problems in oil producer Venezuela.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold was little changed at $393.00 an ounce compared to $392.50 in New York.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040305e0350009u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
