\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar extends rally vs yen, hits 4-month highs.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima   \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to four-month highs against the yen on Friday, extending its month-long rally as optimism grew about U.S. jobs data later in the day and as wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  persisted.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose above 111.20 yen up from around 111.05 yen late in New York on Thursday to probe fresh four-month highs for the fourth consecutive session. It is now nearly six percent above 3-= year lows set last month.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar's remarkable ability to hold above 110 yen after pushing above that psychological level on Tuesday was making the market cautious that Japan might be determined to prevent the dollar's recovery from faltering.   \par \par \uc2 "When the dollar was struggling at lower levels, people blindly sold it on the assumption that the Japanese authorities would not continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the dollar bounced a little. But people aren't so sure anymore," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Such concern was underscored by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data showing Japan's external reserves bulging to $776.86 billion as of end-February, a record high for the sixth straight month and up $35.61 billion from end-January.   \par \par \uc2 The figures were in line with the MOF's announcement a week earlier that its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month had totalled 3.34 trillion yen ($30.07 billion). That brought the total for the first two months of this year above 10 trillion yen - already half of last year's record 20 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as there is this nagging concern about the authorities stepping in again, it now looks like even going below 111 yen would be unlikely for now," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank.   \par \par \uc2 Doubts about how far \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would or could continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were highlighted by comments earlier this week by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2 He said that an improving Japanese economy meant the large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would soon no longer meet the monetary policy needs of Japan.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki defended Japan's stance, telling reporters on Friday that balancing stability and flexibility was important and that he saw no difference between Greenspan's views on Tokyo's policy and Japan's own views.   \par \par \uc2 JOBS DATA   \par \par \uc2 The dollar had its own reasons to be perky amid expectations that the U.S. jobs figures for February would show U.S. employment finally starting to grow significantly, putting the ongoing economic recovery on a firmer footing.   \par \par \uc2 Given that the stubbornly soft jobs market has been one reason why the Federal Reserve has yet to signal any imminent interest rate increases, a strong reading could push bond yields and other market interest rates significantly higher, supporting the dollar, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 "A lot of people are looking for a strong number in the U.S. jobs data today as well," said Tanase at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a rise of 125,000 in February U.S. non-farm payrolls, which will be released at 1330 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady against the dollar hovering near $1.22 after seesaw trading on Thursday, when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, as widely expected.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could retest three-month lows hit this week around $1.2055 soon, depending on how strong the U.S. payroll figures are. ($1=111.08 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040305e0350003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
