\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Dlr/Yen Vols Jump On MOF  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Spree  \b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 20:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options spiked higher Friday in New York as heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities helped push the dollar to new five-month highs against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 This bucked the broader trend in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Friday, which saw the dollar plunge across the board on the back of a startlingly weak U.S. employment report.  \par \par \uc2 Hopes that the rapidly expanding U.S. economy would finally see companies hiring aggressively were dashed by the non-farm payroll report that showed only 21,000 new jobs were created in February. Economists had anticipated the creation of around 125,000 new jobs, according to a Dow Jones Newswires survey.  \par \par \uc2 Not only is this another bad headline number that threatens to undermine the sustainability of the U.S. recovery - December's and January's figures were revised lower, which means average jobs growth is a paltry 42,000 over the past three months - but the dovish implications for U.S. interest rates represent the real worry for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But largely thanks to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is estimated to have bought at least $10 billion in New York trading Friday, the dollar surged against the yen, taking out options-related resistance at Y111.50 to trade above Y112.00.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, which has pushed the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to new five-and-a-half-year lows against sterling and the Australian dollar, is widely thought to constitute a record for a single day. It has catapulted the dollar up into territory not seen since around the time of the Group of Seven meeting of finance ministers and central bankers in Dubai last September.  \par \par \uc2 "People are short gamma and people need protection," said an options dealer at a Japanese bank in New York. "Holding onto long dollar/yen positions has been working and making money but we've come a long way. People fear a very, very deep correction so they need options protection and so short-dated options are very expensive."  \par \par \uc2 Around 2:00 EST (1900 GMT) Friday, in the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money (ATM) dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.0%/9.3%, up from 8.85%/9.15% earlier Friday in Tokyo and 8.7%/9.0% in New York Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities implied by one-week dollar/yen options popped up above 10% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities implied by one-week euro/dollar options were up at 12.6%/13.3% in New York Friday from just under 13%, while one-month options were little changed at 12.0%/12.20% from 12.05%/12.30% in Tokyo Friday and 12.0%/12.3% in New York Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month ATM euro/yen options surged Friday as the spot rate jumped to an eight-month high, with implied volatilities up at 11.25%/12.25% from 10.35%/10.45% in Tokyo Friday and 10.45%/10.75% in New York Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 One-month quarter delta risk-reversals favored dollar call/yen put options by 0.4%/0.7%, compared with 0.35%/0.65% in Tokyo Friday and 0.2%/0.5% in New York Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar calls/euro puts were favored by 0.0%/0.4% against 0.1%/0.4% in Tokyo and 0.2%/0.6% the day before.  \par \par \uc2 Around 2:00 EST (1900 GMT), the dollar was at Y112.20, up from Y111.08 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.2378, up from $1.2190 Thursday in New York and was also up at Y138.88 from Y135.40 Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 05-03-04 1944GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040305e035000on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
