\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Watch: Greenspan  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Critique Makes Tokyo Uneasy  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takeshi Takeuchi \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japanese diplomats here are uneasy about the Federal Reserve chairman's critique this week of Tokyo's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, even though it had little effect on the market and was played down in public by Tokyo officials.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 They are struggling to interpret Greenspan's remarks, and say in private they are concerned that his words could mean the U.S. will soon become less tolerant of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Policy-makers back in Tokyo didn't say much in response to Alan Greenspan's warning Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not be sustainable. Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda, for one, said he didn't think the central banker was being critical of Japan.  \par \par \uc2 But in Washington, where the diplomats are paid to scrutinize U.S. policy, "we feel a little uneasy about the chairman's remarks," a source close to the Japanese diplomatic circle told Dow Jones Newswires. "Often in the past, his seemingly casual remarks have later turned out to be something that set or changed the basic tenet of policies in the ensuing periods."  \par \par \uc2 For several reasons, the Japanese diplomats suspect that might be the case with Greenspan's words this time around too. Among them are Washington's views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy, the timing of Greenspan's remarks, current Japanese economic conditions and U.S. politics.  \par \par \uc2 If the U.S. government is indeed about to become less lenient toward \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that could hurt Tokyo's efforts to keep the yen weak until it is sure the economy's recovery is rock-solid. It might spur Tokyo to speed up its efforts to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  while it still can, before Washington starts grumbling.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Monetary Policy And \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 One element in Greenspan's remarks that hint at future U.S. disapproval of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy is the way the central banker brings up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and monetary policy in the same breath.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From Japanese officials' point of view, a major justification for their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market action is their perception that the market has overvalued the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This way of thinking, however, isn't shared by officials of the U.S. and other industrialized nations. They, for the most part, support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a complement to the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy. Since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has little room left to ease policy further as it keeps interest rates near zero, it's acceptable for Japan to use yen selling to end deflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Greenspan questioned whether this tactic will remain suitable. "We are getting closer to the point where continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at this scale will no longer meet the monetary policy needs of Japan," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.S., as in many other countries, it's the Treasury Department and not the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that decides \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. However, "it is usually the case that comments by Mr. Greenspan, who is in regular contact with Secretary (John) Snow, are made after sufficient coordination with the Treasury's thinking," the source said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S.'s message, therefore, might be: the need to pull down the yen for monetary policy purposes might soon disappear, so stop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another issue is the timing of Greenspan's remarks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They came right after Tokyo launched a series of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that players characterized as trying to push up the dollar's value. In the past, by contrast, Tokyo tended to take action to smooth market moves when the yen strengthened, a tactic of which Japan's trade partners seemed to approve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, when Japanese authorities spent a record Y27 trillion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the thirteen months through January, the aim was clearly to slow the yen's rise. But now, with the dollar having risen to Y111 from Y105 only two weeks ago, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is continuing, suggesting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has shifted its strategy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The way MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the last couple of weeks looks as if they were trying to jack up the yen to a certain level (against the dollar)," a senior trader at a Japanese bank said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan, in effect, may have been wagging his finger at Tokyo policy-makers by making those remarks at a time when Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy tactics were changing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                  Wake-Up Call For Japan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From a purely economic point of view too, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign looks hard to justify to the outside world.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after all, posted annualized economic growth of 7% in the October-December quarter, the fastest pace in 13 years and a better number than those of the U.S. or the Euro-zone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Last year, when the economic recovery still looked fragile, it was easy to gain support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not only from the U.S. but also from other G7 (Group of Seven major) countries," the trader said. "But with the recovery having shown such steadiness and strength, Greenspan probably meant his remark to be a wake-up call for Japan."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And then there are U.S. political considerations, which are impossible to ignore in a presidential election year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another Japanese diplomatic source noted that Greenspan's remarks came almost at the same time that John Kerry, who is critical of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, effectively clinched the Democratic presidential candidacy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His rival, President George W. Bush, might need to address Kerry's criticism that Japan's keeping the yen artificially weak is hurting the U.S. by putting a lid on exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. president wants to avoid the mistake of his father, George H. W. Bush, who many pundits think wasn't re-elected in large part because the public disapproved of his economic policy management. Bush's strategy, therefore, is "to wipe out as much as possible what could become weak spots in the area of economic policy," says the second source.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan, in principle independent as a central banker from the government but on good terms with the Bush administration, might have been sending an advance warning to Japan that the U.S. won't always be sympathetic to its action in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Considering all these factors, Washington-based Japanese diplomats are still thinking about what Greenspan said, and waiting for what Treasury Secretary John Snow, the architect of U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, might say next.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's certainly better to remain vigilant about what Treasury Secretary Snow will say about Japan over the coming weeks before you forget about Greenspan's words," the first source said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takeshi.takeuchi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Takeshi Takeuchi is a Dow Jones Newswires special writer, specializing in U.S.-Asia relations. Previously, he covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and Japanese economic and trade policy and politics for two decades in Tokyo.) [ 05-03-04 0931GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1187 4256  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040305e035000bo\par }\page {\par\