\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Mar 5  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's snap parliamentary election will be held March 21, officials said Friday, leaving little time for the fundamentalist Islamic opposition to mount a campaign offensive against incumbent Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi. The Election Commission also set March 13 as the day candidates must lodge their nominations, resulting in an official campaign period of about one week. ("UPDATE:Malaysia Sets Short Campaign With Mar 21 Poll Date," ran at 0625 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN WATCH: Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's rare criticism of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy this week has left the country's diplomats here worried whether the remarks may presage a change in the U.S.'s leniency toward Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (=\b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit another record high in February as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressed on with its campaign to hobble the yen and help exporters power the economic recovery. ("=2nd UPDATE:Japan Feb Foreign Reserves Hit Another Record," ran at 0242 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Upbeat household spending data and an index of economic indicators released Friday gave further impetus to the view that a sustainable recovery in Japan's economy is finally being realized. ("=UPDATE: Japan Spending Data, Index Confirm Econ Upswing," ran at 0548 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Premier Wen Jiabao served notice Friday that China's government intends to tackle a combination of potentially dangerous over-investment and flat consumption in order to sustain economic growth in 2004. In his first report to the National People's Congress since being promoted to his post a year ago, Wen outlined strategies to boost the income and reduce the tax burden for farmers, still the country's dominant sector. (UPDATE will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Lower House of parliament Friday cleared a Y82.111 trillion draft budget bill for next fiscal year that requires another huge issuance of government debt but keeps spending roughly in line with the current fiscal year. ("Japan Lower House Clears Next FY Budget;Enactment Assured," ran at 0752 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: As sentiment toward the dollar has improved this week, many analysts say a side-effect will be gains for the yen against the euro, a view supported by technical analysis. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Analysts, Charts Bearish Euro, Sterling Vs Yen," ran at 0126 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: A sell-off in Japanese government bonds this week might be expected to have turned investors away from riskier debt. But in fact, their demand for corporate bonds remains strong. High-rated companies issuing bonds this week were able to do so at stable spreads between the coupons they offered and yields on JGBs. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: Corp Bond Sales Smooth Despite Choppy JGBs," ran at 0857 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Residential and nonresidential building activity in New Zealand slowed in the fourth quarter from the third, potentially knocking estimates of economic growth for the last three months of 2003. ("=UPDATE: NZ Building Activity Slowed In 4Q, May Knock GDP," ran at 2347 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Manufacturing activity in Singapore expanded for the ninth consecutive month in February but the Purchasing Managers' Index was down for the second month running, indicating the recovery could run out of steam. ("Singapore Feb PMI 53.1 Vs Jan 53.8;Electronics Index Dips," ran at 1430 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: A barometer of the New Zealand public's confidence in the future has fallen sharply in February, according to a Philips Fox-National Business Review poll published Friday. The poll also confirms other recent political polls that have shown support for the Labour-led government diving and support for the opposition National Party surging after opposition leader Don Brash's keynote speech in January attacking what he says is preferential treatment for Maori. ("NZ Public Confidence Sours, Support For Govt Dives - Poll," ran at 2016 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's consumer price index rose for a second straight month in February due to higher food and oil prices compared with the year-earlier period, the government said Friday. Taiwan's top government statistics agency said the island's consumer price index was up 0.64% in February, following the 0.01% increase in January. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan's Feb CPI Rises For 2nd Straight Month," ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Inflation in the Philippines remained steady in February, but government and private economists said the risks of a build up of inflationary pressure linger due largely to the weakness of the peso. ("UPDATE: Philippine Feb Inflation Steady, But Risks Linger," ran at 0632 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand dollar is overvalued versus major trading partner \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the Australian dollar, and continued strength could cause "lasting damage" to the economy, Finance Minister Michael Cullen said Friday in his latest attempt to jawbone the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. ("NZ Fin Min: NZD Overvalued Vs Trading Partner \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 2001 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: A migration of workers from China's rural areas to the cities will fuel demand for housing and infrastructure, providing opportunities for investment, said Chip Kay, Co-President of Warburg Pincus LLC. (INTERVIEW will run by around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rose to a new four-month peak against the yen in Asia Friday, as the market grew increasingly convinced that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities are serious about keeping the dollar moving upward. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Extends Gains Vs Yen As MOF Gets Serious," ran at 0542 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The initial public offering of Tom Online Inc., the Internet spin-off of tycoon Li Ka-shing's media flagship TOM Group Ltd., was priced at HK$1.51 per share, the top end of the offer's indicative price range. Initial tallies of subscription forms showed that the Hong Kong retail IPO was over 90 times covered, according to a person familiar with the deal. (Story will run by around 0910 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India's biggest ever share sale, the public offer of a 10% stake in exploration firm Oil & Natural Gas Corp., was heavily oversubscribed, indicating that the government's gamble of selling shares in six companies to meet fiscal targets will pay off. The offer of 143 million shares in ONGC, with a price band of 680 to 750 rupees a share, was met with bids for 153 million within 15 minutes of trading Friday on the Bombay Stock Exchange. ("=UPDATE: India ONGC Public Issue Heavily Oversubscribed," ran at 0839 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares of PCCW were suspended from trading pending an announcement in "relation to a major transaction," according to the Hong Kong stock exchange. Hong Kong's largest telecom company said Thursday after announcing an enormous 2003 loss that it is at an advanced stage of transferring its property assets to Hong Kong-listed gas provider Dong Fang Gas. (UPDATE will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian wagering group Tab Ltd. will enter eleventh hour negotiations with two major Sydney horse racing clubs in the hope that its Sky Channel unit can reach a deal over race broadcasting rights. Earlier Friday, the plug was pulled on Sky Channel broadcasts of races conducted by the Australian Jockey Club and Sydney Turf Club because a rights agreement had lapsed without a replacement deal being found. ("=UPDATE: Tab In 11th Hour Talks With Sydney Racing Clubs," ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's tourism industry appears to have avoided the harmful effects of bird flu, though concerns about the disease remain in the region, analysts and industry officials said Friday. ("=Tourists To Malaysia Undeterred By Scare Over Bird Flu," ran at 0649 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Officials of Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co. Friday confirmed parts of a report indicating they are considering new chip plant sites and building a specialized R&D center, but said this forms part of their long-term plans and there is no schedule for the projects at this time. ("TSMC Mulls Chip Plant Sites, R&D Center; No Schedule," first take ran at 0157 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Shares in Kanebo Ltd. surged Friday on local media reports the ailing firm may get a government bailout extending beyond its cosmetics \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=UPDATE:Kanebo Surges On Reports Govt To Give Broader Aid," ran at 0608 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Japan's Rakuten Inc. may not be so popular among investors who prefer a bricks-and-mortar shopping experience, but the country's biggest Internet mall operator has considerable appeal among less tech-averse punters. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Japan's Rakuten Finds Renewed Favor," ran at 0011 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The Australian Shareholders' Association Ltd. on Friday called for the resignation of Aristocrat Leisure Ltd.'s former chairman John Ducker from the board following the gaming group's trading performance. ("UPDATE:Australia Shr Assoc Wants Ducker Off Aristocrat Bd," ran at 0404 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Standard Chartered Bank, which lost a bid for KorAm Bank to Citigroup Inc. last month, will continue to look for investment opportunities to grow its six-month old consumer banking business. But the targets would likely be one of the smaller banks or credit card companies. ("=INTERVIEW: After KorAm Loss, StanChart Lowers Its Sights," ran at 0743 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Sichuan New Hope Agribusiness Co. Ltd., one of China's largest livestock feed producers, expects sales to gradually recover now that the bird flu epidemic appears to be under control, a company executive said. (INTERVIEW will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Pertamina, Indonesia's state-owned oil and gas company, said it has reached an outline agreement with Exxon Mobil Corp. over how to develop the nation's largest oil discovery in three decades. ("=UPDATE: Indonesia, Exxon Mobil Reach Deal On Cepu Field," ran at 0356 GMT) [ 05-03-04 0903GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN POWER: The Japanese government will this summer set out strict rules for the country's electricity transmission network system, a move that is part of its efforts to push ahead with liberalization of the power retail market. ("=JAPAN POWER: Tougher Network Rules A Key To Deregulation," ran at 0519 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD: Pakistan's biggest independent power producer, Hub Power Co., is in talks with the government to secure a long-term gas supply to run its proposed power plants near Karachi, said a senior executive. (INTERVIEW will run by around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: The Malaysia Derivatives Exchange, fresh from the launch of crude palm kernel oil futures, is aiming to introduce trading of options on crude palm oil futures later in the year, said Zaha Rina Zahari, head of exchanges at the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange. ("ODJ INTERVIEW: MDEX Plans Launch Of Options On CPO Futures In 2004," ran at 0457 GMT) [ 05-03-04 0903GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040305e035000ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
