\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Mizoguchi: No Comment On Possible  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pointman Friday continued to emphasize the strength of the U.S. economy, suggesting he feels further dollar gains against the yen are appropriate.  \par \par \uc2 "In the mid-term, it is desirable for (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to reflect basic fundamentals. Excessive moves and volatility are undesirable," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters, adding that the U.S. economy is still fundamentally strong.  \par \par \uc2 The government sold a whopping Y10 trillion in the first two months of this year alone to keep the dollar above Y105 and help exporters power the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 In New York trading overnight, at least one Japanese bank had bids placed for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Y110.50, another reportedly at Y110.70 before at least one other of the ministry's favored agent banks was seen paying dollar offers between Y110.90 and Y111.00.  \par \par \uc2 At 0057 GMT, the dollar was trading around Y111.35.  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale [ 05-03-04 0106GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040305e0350000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
