\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar extends rally vs yen, hits 4-month highs.   \b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 00:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 5 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to four-month highs against the yen on Friday, extending its month-long rally as optimism grew about U.S. jobs data later in the day and as wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  persisted.    \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose above 111.20 yen by 2320 GMT, up from around 111.05 yen late in New York on Thursday, probing fresh four-month highs for the fourth consecutive session. It is now nearly six percent above 3-= year lows set last month.    \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's remarkable ability to hold above 110 yen after pushing above that psychological level on Tuesday was making the market cautious that Japanese authorities might be determined to prevent the dollar's recovery from faltering.    \par \par \uc2  "When the dollar was struggling at lower levels, people blindly sold dollars on the assumption that the Japanese authorities would not continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the dollar bounced a little. But people aren't so sure anymore," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.    \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are looking for a strong number in the U.S. jobs data today as well."    \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters have, on average, forecast a rise of 125,000 in February U.S. non-farm payrolls, which will be released at 1330 GMT.    \par \par \uc2  Given that the stubbornly soft jobs market has been one reason why the Federal Reserve has yet to signal any imminent interest rate increases, a strong reading could push bond yields and other market interest rates significantly higher, supporting the dollar, traders said.    \par \par \uc2  The euro was steady against the dollar hovering near $1.22 after seesaw trading on Thursday, when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, as widely expected.    \par \par \uc2  Traders said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could retest three-month lows hit this week around $1.2055 soon, depending on how strong the U.S. payroll figures are.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040304e034002zb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
