\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-MOF Watanabe says yen/dollar adjustment continues.  \b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 4 (Reuters) - The current adjustment in the yen-dollar exchange rate is not overdone and is likely to continue for some time, a top official of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "It is still in the process of adjustment," Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's international division, told reporters at a regular briefing when asked about the dollar's rise. "It will take a little while longer."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was calmer against the yen on Thursday, and in Tokyo trade was stuck in a narrow range around 110.10 yen , with some traders suspecting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , placing dollar bids around 110 yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen ($182 billion) last year and has sold another 10 trillion yen so far this year due to concerns that the yen's rise could stifle its economy's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is not aimed at setting a specific level for the yen, saying Japan would act to smooth markets if there were big swings in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, to help any exporters who were not hedged.  \par \par \uc2 He also said Japan could act if the yen rate deviated from economic fundamentals but that it did not have a specific level in mind for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Referring to remarks by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan earlier this week that Japan's buying of U.S. assets would have to slow at some point, Watanabe said even if it did, there would be no effect on U.S. interest rates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used most of the dollars it bought through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. Treasuries, effectively capping rises in U.S. rates.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040304e034000zy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
