\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar near multi-month highs on U.S. job hopes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 4 (Reuters) - The dollar inched up on Thursday, keeping in sight of multi-month highs against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on expectations for an upbeat reading in a key U.S. jobs report the following day.  \par \par \uc2 A recovery in the long-sluggish U.S. labour market could herald an eventual rate hike by the Federal Reserve from the current one percent and make the dollar's yield more attractive to investors.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, the euro was hampered by the view that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), widely expected to keep its benchmark rate at 2 percent later in the day, may slash rates in the future to shore up the euro zone's slow growth.  \par \par \uc2 For that reason, the market may pay more attention to what ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet has to say at his press conference at 1330 GMT than to the rate announcement at 1245 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 "If the ECB hints at a future rate cut, the euro will head lower again," said Toshihiro Azuma, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 By 0600 GMT, the euro was around $1.2175 down about a quarter percent from late U.S. levels but off its three-month low of $1.2056 hit in New York on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was flat against the dollar at around $1.8280 after spending much of the day below late-U.S. levels. It stayed off one-month lows near $1.8156.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of England also announces the result of its rate-setting meeting at 1200 GMT. It is expected to leave rates steady.  \par \par \uc2 Still, expectations the bank will raise rates soon to curb a booming housing market and buoyant consumer spending helped to support the pound versus the euro at around 66.65 pence per euro near Wednesday's one-year high around 66.10.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was calmer against the yen, stuck in a narrow range around 110.10 yen little changed on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , placing dollar bids around 110 yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen ($182 billion) last year and has spent another 10 trillion yen so far this year due to concerns that the yen's rise will stifle the country's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hardly flinched, supported by Japan's recent recovery and consequent capital inflows to Japanese shares.  \par \par \uc2 "Roughly speaking, we have a 15 trillion yen trade surplus in Japan and about nine trillion yen in Japanese stocks bought by foreign investors a year," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as there remains such a surplus of dollars against the yen, the dollar is unlikely to rise much against the yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists, whose views were gathered or confirmed on Wednesday, showed that many expected the dollar to fall to 105 yen in three months and 102 in a year's time.  \par \par \uc2 Another Reuters survey also showed a majority see the euro climbing to a record high of $1.30 at some point this year, feeding on the dollar's vulnerable points such as the huge U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some traders say the dollar's two-year decline had run its course, arguing that many market players now need to buy back dollars they had sold aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak-dollar trend has been reversed and the market is looking to buy dollars. Traders will come up with excuses to pick up the dollar," said Yoshiharu Yanagisawa, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at State Street in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar bulls say the U.S. payrolls data due on Friday could offer the best incentive to buy the dollar given that the dormant labour market was the main drag on the U.S. recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The market sees the U.S. weekly jobless claims figures due at 1330 GMT as a precursor to Friday's employment data. Initial claims last week were seen at 345,000, down from 350,000 the week before. ($1=110.04 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040304e034000nk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
