\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Surges Against Yen, Edges up Against Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar strengthened against its major rivals in see-saw trade Thursday, led by gains against the yen after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 111.00 yen for the first time in four months.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave interest rates unchanged at 2% - what the ECB termed an "appropriate" level - was largely expected, so its impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relatively benign.  \par \par \uc2 During a press conference, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet painted a positive picture of domestic and export-led growth, suggesting rates may not be coming down soon. He said inflation could even nudge higher in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 This helped lift the euro up from intraday lows and limited the dollar's gains against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Trichet's refusal to give much away also encouraged dealers to turn their attention towards the U.S. employment report due for release Friday and adjust their positions ahead of the data.  \par \par \uc2 Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York, said Mr. Trichet's performance and the nervous anticipation ahead of the payrolls data Friday were behind the euro's relatively rangebound trading Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "I guess it was just (a case of) settling down from the violent moves in the last few days and waiting for tomorrow," Mr. Brickman said. "Trichet avoided stepping into anything."  \par \par \uc2 The base for the dollar's hefty gains against the yen, however, was solid buying from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ministry appears to be getting more aggressive in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no longer content with supporting the dollar but now taking advantage of its broader bid tone to drive the greenback sharply higher.  \par \par \uc2 At least one Japanese bank had bids placed for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 110.50 yen, another reportedly at 110.70 yen before at least one other of the Ministry's favored agent banks was seen paying dollar offers between 110.90 yen and 111.00 yen.  \par \par \uc2 This opened the way for short-term players to enter the fray and take out options-related resistance at 111.00 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "There was a big option and it just went though it," said the head dealer at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was trading at $1.2190 compared with $1.2212 late Wednesday in New York. Boosted by the dollar's jump against the yen, the euro was also up against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 135.40 yen from 134.38 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was over a yen stronger at 111.08 yen from 110.02 yen late Wednesday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was up a touch at 1.2934 francs from 1.2913 francs, while sterling was quite a bit off at $1.8230 from $1.8308.  \par \par \uc2 The pound's relatively weak performance Thursday was likely down to long liquidation on disappointment from some investors at the Bank of England's decision earlier in the session to leave benchmark interest rates unchanged at 4%. It was not, however, a surprise decision.  \par \par \uc2 As noted, the dollar's biggest gains of the day were against the yen. But while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts at weakening the yen help Japanese exporters dress up their balance sheets ahead of fiscal year-end on March 31, others aren't so pleased.  \par \par \uc2 General Motors Corp.'s chief executive Rick Wagoner Thursday criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts saying it gives Japanese car makers an unfair advantage of hundreds or thousands of dollars on each car they sell in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 10% stronger than it should be, and you're talking about a $25,000 vehicle, it's hundreds or thousands of dollars," said Mr. Wagoner. "It's big money."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, attention is focused primarily on Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls report for February. A very strong number will likely support the dollar, while a weak number could trigger a recovery in the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from recent multi-month lows.  \par \par \uc2 "With ECB President Trichet giving no clues about lower rates it seems that the dollar bulls have lost one argument for a weaker euro," wrote Steve Barrow, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London, in a research note Thursday. "We will have to see whether poor payroll data provides another. If it does we will look to short the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 A survey of economists by Dow Jones Newswires suggests U.S. firms added a net 125,000 workers to their non-farm payrolls in February. They hired an extra 112,000 workers in January but that was below expectations and not enough to sustain the current pace of economic expansion, say economists.  \par \par \uc2 "Anything north of 170,000 would be considered a very strong number and anything below 125,000 considered weakish," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital in New York. Given the relatively disappointing nature of the last few payroll reports and the extent of euro selling in recent weeks, "there is some vulnerability to the upside" for the euro, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Data released Thursday showed that weekly U.S. jobless claims fell 7,000 in the week ended Feb. 28 to a seasonally adjusted 345,000, versus expectations of a 5,000 decline.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley contributed to this report.) [ 03-04-04 1701ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040304e0340006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
