\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Dlr Shorts In Correction Process  \b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 00:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Friday said that the dollar is in the process of correcting from recent weakness, suggesting he believes there is more room for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rebound against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "From a broad point of view it seems that yen-buying, dollar-selling positions are in the process of correcting," Sadakazu Tanigaki said when asked about the dollar's surge to a four-month high of above Y111 overnight.  \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment, however, on what would signify an end to the dollar's correction, saying: "one has to be looking at the markets" to make that call.  \par \par \uc2 At 0003 GMT Friday, the dollar was trading at Y111.20. [ 05-03-04 0010GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities, claiming that the dollar's slide to a three-year low of Y105.16 earlier this year was fundamentally unjustified, have been spending massive amounts of money in markets to help the dollar correct.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting through the Bank of Japan, is suspected of having had a hand in the dollar's overnight surge above Y111, according to traders.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments Friday indicate the ministry may continue acting in markets if it feels that fundamentals are still not being properly reflected. Some in the market say the government is aiming to bring the dollar back near Y115 - a level at which MOF feels "unjustified" yen strength began.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki also said that he believes that comments earlier this week by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy were made more in the context of monetary policy, rather than specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels or conditions.  \par \par \uc2 "In that context, I don't think his remarks are that different from our way of thinking" on monetary policy, Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 But he was quick to add that Japan remains in deflation, and that the government and Bank of Japan must continue to take steps to fight price falls.  \par \par \uc2 "Various economic indicators are showing improvement... but we haven't overcome deflation yet," Tanigaki said, suggesting the government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen as means of helping to ease deflationary pressure on the economy.  \par \par \uc2 In remarks to the Economic Club of New York Tuesday, Greenspan said that "one factor boosting the yen is a significant yen bias on the part of Japanese investors."  \par \par \uc2 He added that this home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bias creates a demand for yen despite low returns, forcing monetary authorities to spend vast amounts to slow yen appreciation and keep the country's exports competitive on the world market.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan added that such a policy is not only problematic, but unsustainable.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson and Bennett Richardson [ 05-03-04 0125GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040304e034000zk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
