\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: GM CEO Criticizes  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Lee Hawkins Jr. and Neal E. Boudette \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ST. TROPEZ, France (Dow Jones)--General Motors Corp.'s (GM) chief executive on Thursday criticized Japan's recent efforts to lower the value of the yen, saying it gives Japanese car makers an unfair advantage of hundreds or thousands of dollars on each car they sell in the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 In an interview here, Rick Wagoner said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases of billions of dollars in international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets lowers the cost of Japanese-made engines and other components that companies like Totyota Motor Corp. (TM) use in cars assembled in their U.S. plants.  \par \par \uc2 "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 10% stronger than it should be, and you're talking about a $25,000 vehicle, it's hundreds or thousands of dollars," Wagoner said at a test drive of the redesigned Opel Astra hatchback, GM's top-selling car in Europe. "It's big money."  \par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material are available on The Wall Street Journal's Web site, WSJ.com.)  \par \par \uc2 Wagoner's harsh words come after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan called \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "awesome." In January and February, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $100 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, mostly to buy dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Wagoner said these purchases and others in 2003 "artificially lower" the value of the yen against the dollar, and tilt the playing field in the U.S. in favor of Japanese car makers.  \par \par \uc2 "We live in a world where trade barriers are coming down," he said. "It's the responsibility of governments to make sure there's fair opportunity." Japan's invtervention "creates a tough situation for companies like ours," he said. "These are formidable competitors They don't need that kind of help."  \par \par \uc2 Although Toyota manufactures many models in the U.S., it still imports from Japan large components such as engines and transmissions that make up a big portion of a car's value. On some models, 50% or less of the value of the vehicles is sourced in North America. On average, about 90% of the value of GM's U.S. vehicles is made in North America.  \par \par \uc2 Wagoner said the lower yen also gives Japanese auto makers an advantage in Europe, especially in view of the euro's present strength. Japanese brands have been gaining share in Europe at a time that profits have plunged for several European-based manufacturers. But Wagoner also criticized the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying its reluctance to cut rates is limiting growth at a time when Europe is suffering from excess manufacturing capacity and high unemployment.  \par \par \uc2 "I continue to be concerned about a lack of commitment to economic growth in Europe. I just don't understand the policy of the ECB of keeping interest rates relatively high," he said. "The ECB, the politicians don't seem to see the importance of economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 In Germany and other countries, environmentalists, some unions and other interest groups see limiting growth as a way of minimizing pollution and stopping the widening of the gap between rich and poor.  \par \par \uc2 GM's European division is striving to break even this year after five years of losses. GM Europe expects unit sales to rise 3% or more this year, somewhat faster than the overall market, said Carl Peter Forster, the head of Opel, GM Europe's main brand.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lee Hawkins Jr., The Wall Street Journal; 313-226-1234  \par \par \uc2 -By Neal E. Boudette, The Wall Street Journal Europe; 49-69-2972-5551 [ 03-04-04 1544ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020040304e034000nm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
