\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar spikes up as U.S. job hopes fuel buy-back.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 3 (Reuters) - The dollar hit multi-month highs against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, maintaining its recent bull run on growing optimism about a pick-up in the long dormant U.S. jobs market.  \par \par \uc2 While expectations for a strong reading in U.S. payroll data due on Friday boosted the greenback, traders also said much of the buying was technically driven short-covering, after the euro fell below key resistance levels versus the dollar on Tuesday. The dollar has now reclaimed more than six percent from last month's record lows against the euro and five percent from 3-= year troughs versus the yen, leading some analysts to declare that its two-year downtrend may be over.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the picture clearly changed yesterday," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "It's becoming clearer that the dollar has bottomed out."  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell about 0.3 percent to $1.2180 extending Tuesday's huge loss of 2.35 cents - its biggest single-day loss in absolute terms since the launch of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  five years ago.  \par \par \uc2 The euro briefly fell as low as a three-month trough of $1.2147.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that the euro fell below resitance around $1.2350 had a huge impact, as some people had thought it would rebound there as it had done a few times before," said Satoshi Tokuda, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the euro was unlikely to get help if the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrains from cutting rates at its policy meeting on Thursday. By 0646 GMT, the dollar was at 110.15 yen up slightly on the day and within shouting distance of Tuesday's four-month highs around 110.40 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The British pound lost more than 0.5 percent to hit a one-month low around $1.8290 The Australian dollar tumbled nearly two percent at one stage to hit two-month low of $0.7485.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar rose above 1.30 Swiss francs for the first time in three months.  \par \par \uc2 A jump in the employment component of Monday's Institute of U.S. Supply Management report bolstered expectations of a strong reading in Friday's non-farm payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 NO MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 Traders were scrambling to buy back dollars they had sold during the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year bear market on the widening U.S current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts said the deficit could shrink or at least stop growing soon.  \par \par \uc2 "It's said it takes about two years for a change in the exchange rate to have an impact on the current account balance. If so, the dollar no longer needs to fall," said Takeda of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 As for the yen, Japan's heavy yen-selling since last year may have finally borne fruit, some analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "With Japan having sold 10 trillion yen so far this year, the demand-supply balance of the market seems to have changed," said Takeda.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, after it sold a record 20 trillion yen ($182 billion) last year, in an effort to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising rapidly and damaging the nation's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Market sentiment has changed so much that the dollar showed little reaction to Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's rare straight-forward comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The current performance of the Japanese economy suggests that we are getting closer to the point where continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the present scale will no longer meet the monetary policy needs of Japan," Greenspan told the Economic Club of New York.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief also said it seemed likely that Tokyo's rate of accumulation of dollar assets "will have to slow at some point and eventually cease" once the current situation of declining prices, or deflation, is past.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan also said that U.S. interest rates must rise eventually.  \par \par \uc2 "The Federal Funds rate is accommodative and at some point it will have to rise back to a more neutral state, because it is inconsistent with general long-term stability," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040303e03300182\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
