\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar seen bursting higher despite Japan selling.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 3 (Reuters) - Neither a recent surge in Tokyo share prices nor selling by Japanese exporters, bringing home profits made abroad, is likely to stop the dollar from rising further after it cleared the key 110 yen level, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts are convinced that the dollar has finally bottomed out after a two-year downtrend and will likely head higher to test its next resistance seen around 112 yen despite expected sales by exporters - key sellers of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "Exporters' selling will probably slow the dollar's rise but it won't be great enough to stop it," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "Many (exporters) have already booked in rates for the fiscal year-end (on March 31) and the volume of sales by those who have yet to do so is likely to be smaller than speculators' dollar demand after a long period of dollar-selling."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar sank to a three-and-a-half-year low around 105.15 yen on February 11 but has rebounded about five percent since then, hitting a four-month high of 110.42 yen on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar has become resilient in the face of negative news and is showing clear signs of a real recovery," said Katsunori Kitakura, treasury department manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, in his strongest criticism yet of Asian central banks' heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was nearing a point where the scale of its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at odds with policy needs.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan also said it seemed likely that Tokyo's rate of accumulation of U.S. assets would have to slow at some point and eventually cease once deflation was past.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke also said that exchange rates should be clearly fixed or fully floating.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market would have taken the series of comments as a "buy yen" factor a few weeks ago but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown little reaction, hovering around 110.20 yen in late Tokyo afternoon trade on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about the Greenspan comment, Japan's top government spokesman, Yasuo Fukuda, said he did not view it as criticism of Tokyo's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to act appropriately when there were sharp swings in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 NEXT TARGET 112 YEN  \par \par \uc2 In 2003, Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($181.7 billion) to curb the rise in the yen and has continued its heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, spending another 10 trillion yen in January and February.  \par \par \uc2 In the near term, analysts say the market is unlikely to push up the yen again given Japan's determination, especially with Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Toshihiko Fukui seen to be sharing the same concerns over the yen's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya said that even a further rally in Tokyo's Nikkei share average, which rose to a 21-month high this week, would provide no help for the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "A rise in share prices will not lead to yen-buying," he said. "Mr Fukui has signalled clearly he does not want a strong yen, so foreign investors will hedge any Japanese stock purchases."  \par \par \uc2 Many economists took a surprise monetary easing by the BOJ on January 20 as a sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shared the same concern over yen strength as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Though few analysts forecast a rise in the dollar above 115 yen, many say the greenback is likely to test its next key level around 112 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a big chance the dollar will try to move above 112 yen and fill the window," said Chuo Mitsui's Kitakura, referring to the open gap of about one and a half yen on the daily chart between September 19 and 22.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar began trading on September 22, a Monday, at around 112.40 yen compared with September 19's late New York level of 114 yen, after the Group of Seven countries, at a meeting in Dubai, called for more flexibility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 If the dollar fills that gap it is likely to rise to around 115 yen, technical analysts say. ($1=110.04 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040303e03300179\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
