\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits 3-month high versus euro on job hopes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 3 (Reuters) - The dollar soared to three-month highs against the euro for the second straight day on Wednesday, maintaining its recent bullish momentum amid growing optimism about a pickup in the long dormant U.S. jobs market.  \par \par \uc2 While expectations for a strong reading in U.S. job data due on Friday boosted the greenback, traders also said much of the buying was technically driven short-covering, after the euro fell below key resistance levels on Tuesday. The dollar has now reclaimed more than six percent from last month's record lows against the euro and five percent from 3-= year troughs versus the yen, leading some analysts to declare that its two-year downtrend may be over.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the picture clearly changed yesterday," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "It's becoming clearer that the dollar has bottomed out."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar jumped to around $1.2170 to the euro in early trade, up about 0.4 percent from late on Tuesday, when it gained 2.35 cents to log its biggest single-day gain in absolute terms since the launch of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  five years ago. The dollar also rose to 110.28 yen up about 0.2 percent on the day and within shouting distance of Tuesday's four-month highs around 110.40 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "There was a lot of stop-loss euro selling. I think the euro could fall another two cents or even four cents," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As of 0301 GMT, the euro was down 0.3 percent on the day around $1.2185 and the dollar was up about 0.1 percent around 110.15 yen.  \par \par \uc2 A jump in the employment component of Monday's Institute of U.S. Supply Management report bolstered expectations of a strong reading in Friday's non-farm payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 NO MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 Traders were scrambling to buy back dollars they had sold during the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year bear market on the widening U.S current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts said the deficit could shrink or at least stop growing soon.  \par \par \uc2 "It's said it takes about two years for a change in the exchange rate to have an impact on the current account balance. If so, the dollar no longer needs to fall," said Takeda of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 As for the yen, Japan's heavy yen-selling since last year may have finally borne fruit, some analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "With Japan having sold 10 trillion yen so far this year, the demand-supply balance of the market seems to have changed," said Takeda.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, after it sold a record 20 trillion yen ($182 billion) last year, in an effort to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising rapidly and damaging the nation's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Market sentiment has changed so much that the dollar showed little reaction to Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's rare straight-forward comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The current performance of the Japanese economy suggests that we are getting closer to the point where continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the present scale will no longer meet the monetary policy needs of Japan," Greenspan told the Economic Club of New York.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief also said it seemed likely that Tokyo's rate of accumulation of dollar assets "will have to slow at some point and eventually cease" once the current situation of declining prices, or deflation, is past.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, Fed Governor Ben Bernanke also said on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates should be either clearly fixed or fully floating. ($1=110.04 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040303e033000ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
