\par\par \b \uc2 GM CEO Calls Japan's Efforts To Weaken Yen "Unfair" -BBC  \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--General Motors (GM) Chief Executive Rick Wagoner has said Japan's efforts to weaken the yen have given it an "unfair" trade advantage, the British Broadcasting Corp. reported on its Web site Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Wagoner told the BBC that repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had left the yen "artificially" low against the dollar and euro.  \par \par \uc2 He said the weak yen had put added pressure on U.S. and European carmakers, struggling against tepid demand. GM's European \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been hit hard, with sales down 10% in January, the report said.  \par \par \uc2 "The amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at record levels, " Wagoner said. "That is keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak against the dollar and the euro, and really reducing the competitive position of the manufacturers' base in Europe and the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 "We think it's actually an unfair trade practice."  \par \par \uc2 Wagoner's comments come as Japanese carmakers are making deep inroads into the U.S. market, with Toyota overtaking third-ranked Chrysler in terms of market share last year, the report said.  \par \par \uc2 The car industry in the U.S. has responded by offering cheap financing deals, a tactic that has kept the showrooms busy at the expense of vastly reduced profit margins, the report said.  \par \par \uc2 "Certainly the competitive situation in the U.S. is tough. I think the heavy level of incentives and other merchandising costs have affected profitability for all of us," Wagoner said.  \par \par \uc2 "We're hoping as the economy picks up we'll see an easing of those incentives, but frankly we'll have to see how that plays out."  \par \par \uc2 However, Wagoner said cheap financing deals had "helped hold up or grow market share during a tough period."  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, Wagoner expects a range of new models, including the updated Vauxhall Astra, to help turn around GM's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  there this year.  \par \par \uc2 He also said GM would continue to push into the booming Chinese car market, where the company recorded a 50% jump in sales last year.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not without risk, but I think the balance of risk versus reward has been very much in favor of reward, so we remain very aggressive in our profile for China." [ 03-03-04 0101GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040303e0330006a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
