\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Jobs, Rates, Dollar Upset Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Markets In A Muddle Over What's Positive \par  \par DJIA          10591.48   loss 88.66     dn 0.8% \par NASDAQ        2039.65    loss 18.15     dn 0.9% \par S&P 500       1149.10    loss 6.87      dn 0.6% \par Dow Future    10581.00   loss 7.00      dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1472.50    loss 0.50         0.0% \par S&P Future    1148.25    loss 0.75      dn 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.06% up 0.07 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A spate of profit taking sent stocks lower Tuesday, cutting into the previous session's gains and dimming hopes that Wall Street would shake its current malaise. Investors, skittish about a stronger dollar and the specter of an interest rate hike, consolidated their gains from Monday's strong session. Treasurys fell on worries about the dollar's newly found strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: With very little economic or corporate news to encourage buyers, and with stock prices already considered somewhat overvalued, there was no real impetus to continue the buying from Monday's session, analysts said, although the market's foundation remains sound.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think what we're seeing here is an interruption in the overall direction of the market," said Peter Cardillo, chief strategist at S.W. Bach & Co.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said nothing to ease Wall Street's concerns, especially about interest rates. He focused his discussion on monetary exchange policy, and said broad \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the dollar was unsustainable.  \par \par \uc2 "The markets may have been more sensitive to interest rates and the dollar than we may have thought," said Jeff Kleintop, chief investment strategist for PNC Financial Services Group. "We also have earnings growth past the peak here, oil prices staying stubbornly high and inflows into equity mutual funds really trailing off. That all contributes to what we're seeing here."  \par \par \uc2 Wall Street has been particularly uneasy about the possibility of higher interest rates since the Fed earlier this year seemed to be backing away from assurances that it would keep rates steady.  \par \par \uc2 With many investors waiting for the government's employment report on Friday, analysts were trying to determine if the market had any momentum that could carry it forward after Monday's rally. That advance was the first real sign of life in a market that has been listless for several weeks.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is going to position itself over the next two days in anticipation of Friday's granddaddy employment report," Cardillo said. "I think we have solid economic expansion, and the market is anticipating continued growth in the labor market."  \par \par \uc2 The rate of job cuts slowed in February, said John Challenger, chief executive of recruiting firm Challenger, Gray & Christmas.  \par \par \uc2 In politics, Massachusetts Sen. John Kerry, making the Democratic presidential nomination his own, promised cheering supporters Tuesday night, "We will win this election and we will build one America of freedom and fairness for all."  \par \par \uc2 "Change is coming to America," vowed the four-term senator, who heads into an eight-month general election campaign leading President Bush in the nationwide polls.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys fell Tuesday on concerns that a stronger dollar will eventually curb buying from Asian foreign central banks and on remarks from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that U.S. interest rates will eventually move higher.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were quick to point out that despite the losses, which moderated in late afternoon trade, prices remain stuck in a narrow trading range that is likely to hold until Friday's employment report if not beyond.  \par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, I think a lot hinges on this employment report," said Brian Edmonds, head of U.S. government debt trading at Banc of America Securities in New York, who believes a weak payrolls number will push the 10-year yield out of its current trading range toward 3.75%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Dollar's Strength Grips Japan Markets \par  \par USD-Yen             110.19       gain  0.10    up 0.1% \par AUD-USD             0.7508       loss  0.0127  dn 1.6% \par Nikkei 225          11379.28     gain  17.77   up 0.2% \par Hang Seng           13427.20     loss  304     dn 2.2% \par JGB Yield           1.4150% up 0.0400 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks traded in a narrow range Wednesday morning as buying encouraged by the dollar's strength was blunted by profit-taking.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: The strengthening of the dollar gave a boost to shares in some Japanese exporters, said Hiroichi Nishi, deputy product manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Morning winners included consumer electronics giant Canon and tiremaker Bridgestone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains by the dollar against the yen mean companies in Japan's export sector earn more from their sales in the United States.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the market lost steam as some investors sought to lock profits a day after the benchmark index closed at its highest market since ending at 11,449.44 on June 11, 2002.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The broader TOPIX index did slightly better in morning trading than the blue-chip Nikkei, helped by gains by banks, which have drawn interest in recent sessions from buyers betting on a recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: The yield on Japan's benchmark 10-year government bond rose to 1.4150% early Wednesday from 1.3750% Tuesday. Its price fell 0.35 to 98.99 points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took trading cues from a weakening yen and sluggish demand for the new issue of 10-year JGB bonds at Tuesday's auction.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Analysts were divided on the key question of whether the dollar's recent robust gains heralded a new and lasting phase of strength for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or simply a short-term correction that would soon give way to renewed declines.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While most analysts stuck with the latter view, some market players - notably Japanese exporters - were holding out for more gains, and in the process helping prolong the dollar's rebound.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Many exporters are likely to sit back and wait (to sell dollars for yen) until the rally looks like it's played out, and that hasn't happened yet," a Tokyo brokerage dealer said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar fell after the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: ECB Rate Cut Hopes Fade, Sapping Mkts \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2167      loss  0.0040  dn 0.4% \par Stlg-USD            1.8310      loss  0.0090  dn 0.5% \par USD-Franc           1.3010      gain  0.0054  up 0.4% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are expected to open slightly lower, although the euro's losses should curb declines, while prices of European government bonds tend lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are headed for a lower opening, with concerns about interest rates and the euro likely to add to some profit-taking that alredy trimmed gains in equities on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spreadbettor IG Index is calling the FTSE down 13 points to 4527 at the open. It expects the DAX to open 20 points lower at 4080 and the CAC 11 down at 3774.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It could prove to be a quiet couple of days before interest rate announcements from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of England on Thursday and the U.S. employment data on Friday," commented Geoff Langham, head of trading at London based CMC Group.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the news, Bank of America Corp. (BAC) disclosed that Italian authorities are investigating the activities of three current or former employees in connection with the Parmalat Finanziera SpA. (PRF.MI) probe.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's Ambev and Belgium's Interbrew plan to announce today a two-stage merger that could displace Anheuser-Busch Cos. as the world's largest brewer by volume  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Prices of European government bonds are likely to open lower, in sympathy with Treasurys and on doubts the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will follow the Bank of Canada's rate-cutting lead.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Canada started the ball rolling Tuesday with a 25-basis-point interest rate cut, but the market believes the ECB will leave its own rates steady when it meets Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And it's this fading rate cut optimism that contributed to profit-taking in bunds Tuesday, along with a weaker euro. Gilts, meanwhile, outperformed with the help of a weaker U.K. survey on retail sales.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts at Commerzbank expect the ECB to leave rates unchanged for the rest of the year, see the short end as expensive and recommend selling June Euribor futures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategists at UBS also see a risk of a rise in short yields in conjunction with some curve flattening.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Bonds are facing a triple risk," said one London trader. "There's the ECB announcement itself, the potential for negative comments in the press conference afterwards and then of course (U.S.) payrolls on Friday."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The short end will be especially sensitive to comments from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet and whether he reiterates that the current level of rates is appropriate, he added.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session on a weaker mode, and $1.1940 seems a likely target for the euro in the meantime, without erasing its underlyinhg bullish trend. Gold's spot price tumbled in response to the dollar's recovery, targeting support at around $390 an ounce.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the dramatic nature of the dollar's move was fueled by technical factors, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is also reflecting jitters heading into Thursday's ECB rate-setting meeting, despite consensus expectations for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold its key interest rate steady at 2%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "No one really wanted to be long euros before the ECB to begin with," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. "Basically there is nothing to be gained from it."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the eighth straight month, ECB watchers polled by Dow Jones Newswires were unanimous in saying they don't expect any move on rates. Additionally, the 28 respondents expect euro-zone rates to hold steady through June, with no shift in its monetary policy bias.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors evidently won't believe that until they see it and argue that any hint of an upcoming ECB rate cut would undermine some of the yield advantage of holding the euro relative to the dollar. [ 03-03-04 0058ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan warned that Japan's efforts to prevent its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising against the dollar have resulted in an "awesome" accumulation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves that could soon become "problematic" for the world's second-largest economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think Greenspan fired a shot across \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bow," said Rebecca Patterson, senior global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. "He wasn't saying they should stop \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but he was suggesting the pace of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not sustainable and also questioning if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recovery at this point requires that much \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Short-term speculative investors are making money on this," said Patterson. "They think that this move has some momentum and over the last couple of days, they've become short-term dollar bulls. It means they're going to ride this move for as long as it's there."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut its bellwether overnight rate to 2.25%, the second easing this year and the fourth since the rate peaked at 3.25% in mid-2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Wednesday, March 3: Services Indices, Beige Book \par GMT/Country/Indicator/Event \par 0030  US           Fed Gov Bernanke speaks on money, gold \par 0850  FRA  Feb     PMI Services Survey \par 0855  GER  Feb     PMI Services Survey \par 1200  US   Feb 28  MBA Refinancing Index \par 1500  US   Feb     ISM Services Index \par 1900  US           Fed Reserve Beige Book \par 2130  US           Treasury Secy Snow speaks in Washington \par 2350  JPN          Weekly Portfolio Flows Data   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker, Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 03-03-04 0058ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020040303e033000b8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
