\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Lower; Poor Tuesday Auction Weighs  \b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bond prices ended sharply lower Wednesday, with sentiment unable to recover from poor 10-year JGB auction results Tuesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Lead 10-year JGB futures ended 0.43 lower at 138.51, while the yield of the cash 10-year JGBs rose four basis points to 1.425%.  \par \par \uc2 Mild bargain-hunting helped lift the bond market off intraday lows, but wasn't enough to turnaround the tattered sentiment, traders said. The lead contract's intraday low of 138.26 was its lowest level since Jan. 9, while the 10-year yield briefly rose to a three-month high of 1.44%.  \par \par \uc2 Tuesday's poor auction highlighted that bond market players won't continue ignoring robust share prices and Japan's mild economic recovery, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Although the Nikkei 225 Stock Average fluctuated around Tuesday's close this session, it still was near its highest level since June 2002. The yen's weakness against the dollar overnight also depressed bond prices, traders added.  \par \par \uc2 "The environment surrounding the bond market has changed over the past few sessions," said Jun Ishii, chief strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 While market participants found the 10-year yield of 1.19%-1.2% incompatible with stronger shares and a further steady recovery of the economy, they have yet to find appropriate levels for the yield, Ishii said.  \par \par \uc2 The lead bond futures contract has lost almost Y2 during five consecutive sessions of declines after hitting a recent high of 140.45.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices as of 0600 GMT: \par  \par    Tiffe Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Price:       99.855  Dn 0.010 \par    SGX Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Price:         99.850  Dn 0.015 \par    TSE Mar 10Y JGB Future Price:        138.51  Dn 0.43 \par    SGX Mar 10Y JGB Future Price:        138.50  Dn 0.33 \par    10-Yr 1.3% JGB No. 258 Yield:        1.425%  Up 0.040 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-03-04 0155ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rises in long-term yields drove rates of short- to medium-term bonds sharply higher Wednesday, traders said. The yield of the newest five-year bonds rose 4.5 basis points to 0.58%. New five-year bonds to be auctioned next week were traded at 0.625% in the when-issued market, five basis points up from Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts expect the higher yields in the short end of the curve to eventually stabilize, as the Bank of Japan's ultra-easy policy will remain intact for the time being, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What is different now from the rises in bond yields in August is that expectations of the continuation of the BOJ's ultra-easy policy haven't been rattled yet," Ishii said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, he said it is very unlikely the BOJ will tightening policy before April 2005, when Japan lifts its blanket guarantees on bank deposits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Koji Shimamoto, chief strategist at BNP Paribas, said investors are unlikely to start buying bonds before the March end of the fiscal year, so the 10-year yield is likely to rise to the higher end of the 1.3%-1.5% range this month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the yield will start sliding again once the new fiscal year starts, as investors' fresh money flows in, Shimamoto said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the money market, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold three-month financing bills with the lowest price of 99.9975 to yield 0.0099%, the highest level since 0.0129% at an auction in October. The yield was 0.0059% at the previous auction last week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Along with falling JGBs, market participants expect the size of financing bill issuance to increase in coming months following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's heavy yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in January and February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, rates on commercial paper weren't affected by the high volatility in the JGB market thanks to the short supply of the paper, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Excluding asset-backed commercial paper, it is estimated around Y3.6 trillion of the paper will mature in March, but players expect new issues to be less than Y3 trillion, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen interest rate swaps rose across the board amid sharp rises of JGB yields, traders say. Two-year swaps rose 3.25 basis points to 0.2025%, five-year swaps rose 4.75 basis points to 0.6675%, and 10-year swaps were 4.25 basis points higher at 1.385%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although rates were higher, some Japanese banks were interested in receiving fixed rates in short to medium-term zones, traders noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Michiyo Seki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; michiyo.seki@dowjones.com (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this report.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Gibson [ 03-03-04 0318ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040303e033000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
