\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  seen delaying BOJ policy exit.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 2 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made it difficult for the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to abandon its three-year-old super-loose monetary policy even when the time seems ripe to do so.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say snowballing issuance of financing bills to fund Japan's dumping of the yen is straining its already giant public debts and that investors may be unable to absorb those FBs if the bank stops pumping ample funds into the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell yen, it will have to sell so many FBs that the Bank of Japan may have to delay ending its quantitative easing even when the economic environment warrants it," said Hiroyuki Kubota, bond markets director at Research and Pricing Technologies Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The government, fearing yen strength could dent the country's exports, has beefed up its issuance of three-month FBs. It plans to raise the weekly offering to 5.3-5.5 trillion yen ($48.6-50.5 billion) from April, compared with 3.8 trillion yen a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the weekly issuance to reach six to seven trillion yen soon as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has to issue FBs to finance past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it stops stepping in to sell the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But interest rates on three-month FBs remain stable because investors have poured their idle funds into the instruments as a result of the BOJ's massive money market fund injections.  \par \par \uc2 Those funds have failed to penetrate the private sector as companies are busy writing off bad debts and banks are afraid of not getting back their loans to firms.  \par \par \uc2 What's more, the BOJ helps to absorb FBs by conducting outright purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But analysts are worried about what could happen when the economy turns around, banks start increasing lending and the BOJ wants to end its zero-rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as the BOJ keeps its quantitative easing, the problem of quickly increasing FB issuance will be under control as the BOJ is committed to helping to buy them," said Izuru Katoh, chief economist at Totan Research.  \par \par \uc2 "But who would buy a gigantic volume of FBs if the BOJ started considering ending the quantitative easing policy?"  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the three-month FBs edged higher on Monday to 0.006 percent, from 0.002 percent in early February.  \par \par \uc2 CAUSING FISCAL STRAIN  \par \par \uc2 Analysts are concerned about fiscal strain from FB issuance, with Japan having spent about 10.5 trillion yen in the first two months of this year - more than half the annual record of 20 trillion yen in 2003 - to slow the yen's appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Official data shows FBs for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds totalled 65.5 trillion yen at the end of September - more than the annual U.S. current account deficit - and analysts estimate the amount to have risen sharply to 85 trillion yen by the end of February.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say this could have an unhealthy impact on the short-term interest rate market, and say any damage there would have an immediate adverse effect on long-term JGBs.  \par \par \uc2 Such worries have grown as Japan faces sizable maturities of long-dated JGBs falling in 2006 to 2008.  \par \par \uc2 "All of which makes it hard to come up with a soft-landing scenario for Japan involving a solution to the huge debt problem, and this means the BOJ will stay pressured to keep its ultra-easy policy," Totan's Katoh said.  \par \par \uc2 And if the BOJ maintains quantitative easing even while the economy fully recovers, Japan could be hit by hyper-inflation, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "What happens when interest rates stay too low compared with real economic growth is inflation - extreme inflation," said Kazuhiko Sano, chief strategist at Nikko Citigroup. ($1=108.95 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040302e032000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
