\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Soars, as Technical Levels Break Down  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Grainne McCarthy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- Uncertainty about the direction of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates teamed up with the breach of key technical levels to send the dollar soaring against its principal rivals Tuesday, hitting U.S. stocks and bonds in the process and sparking caution about the near-term outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Having already started the global day on a positive note, the dollar catapulted higher against the euro late morning in New York after shattering key single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support around $1.2350, eventually pushing the euro as low as $1.2199, its weakest level since Dec. 15 and sharply down from its high for the day at $1.2468.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, the euro was trading at $1.2216, compared with $1.2445 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at 110.10 yen, up more than a full yen from 109.05 yen late Monday, having traded as high as 110.49 yen. Sterling was trading at $1.8395, after tumbling to a one-month low at $1.8355 from $1.8670 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The price of gold, which typically moves in the opposite direction to the dollar, also plummeted, falling $6.50 an ounce to $393.30.  \par \par \uc2 While the dramatic nature of the dollar's move was fueled by technical factors, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is also reflecting jitters heading into Thursday's ECB rate-setting meeting despite consensus expectations for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold its key interest rate steady at 2%.  \par \par \uc2 "No one really wanted to be long euros before the ECB to begin with," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. "Basically there is nothing to be gained from it."  \par \par \uc2 For the eighth-straight month, ECB watchers polled by Dow Jones Newswires were unanimous in saying they don't expect any move on rates. Additionally, the 28 respondents expect euro-zone rates to hold steady through June, with no shift in its monetary-policy bias.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors evidently won't believe that until they see it, and argue that any hint of an upcoming ECB rate cut would undermine some of the yield advantage of holding the euro relative to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also shored up the dollar against the yen, with the greenback trading above 110 yen for the first time in four months. The massive amounts Japan has been spending in its attempt to stop a strong yen from derailing its export-led recovery drew unusually frank criticism Tuesday from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, which sent the dollar down modestly from its intraday highs against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In comments before a luncheon hosted by the Economic Club of New York, he warned Japan's efforts to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising against the dollar have resulted in an "awesome" accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that could soon become "problematic" for the world's second-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 "I think Greenspan fired a shot across \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bow," said Rebecca Patterson, senior global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan in New York. "He wasn't saying they should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but he was suggesting the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not sustainable and also questioning if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovery at this point requires that much \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Still, the bulk of the broad move higher in the dollar came before Mr. Greenspan began to speak in New York, and to a large extent was a result of short-term investors unwinding positions that had been based on the dollar heading weaker and options resistance to a stronger dollar simply breaking down.  \par \par \uc2 "Short-term speculative investors are making money on this," said Ms. Patterson. "They think that this move has some momentum and over the last couple of days, they've become short-term dollar bulls. It means they're going to ride this move for as long as it's there."  \par \par \uc2 Along with the ECB meeting, the February U.S. employment report looms large at the end of this week, and a surprisingly strong number could certainly extend the correction. A strong report would also arguably get \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors thinking about the timing of a Fed rate hike.  \par \par \uc2 "The federal funds rate is accommodative ... but at some point, it will have to rise to a more neutral state because it's inconsistent with general long-term stability," Mr. Greenspan said Tuesday, in comments that could intensify that guessing game even further. He added, "Central bankers have a primary role to protect the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Low interest rates relative to the rest of the world have been a key factor weighing on the dollar over the past year. Many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets think the dollar is set to remain constrained by low Federal Reserve interest rates, but right now momentum is working in the greenback's favor.  \par \par \uc2 In the Treasurys market, while intuitively dollar strength should be beneficial, prices reacted to the possibility that sustained dollar strength will remove a key source of support from the market. Along with Japan, other Asian governments have been major buyers of Treasurys for months, as they park dollars bought via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. government bonds.  \par \par \uc2 North of the border meanwhile, the Canadian dollar dipped only modestly, avoiding the fate of many of the dollar's other rivals and shrugging off the impact of a one-quarter percentage-point cut in Canada's bellwether overnight rate by the Bank of Canada to 2.25%. Late in the day, the U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3420, up from C$1.3395 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The rate cut was the second this year.  \par \par \uc2 -By Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this article) [ 03-02-04 1653ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1033 1865 20714 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040302e03200018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
