\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Stronger Dollar Can't Crack Treasury Range  \b0\par\par\uc2 880 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If Tuesday's record is anything to go by, it seems the U.S. Treasury market takes its cues much more from a weaker than a stronger dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Sure, yields on the benchmark 10-year note moved higher on from levels late Monday as some concern emerged that flows from Japan's government into U.S. government securities could slow as a result of the dollar's strong comeback. But Treasurys remain ensconced in a protective trading range that has kept volatility in other financial markets mostly at bay.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar jumped well above Y110 on Tuesday as market participants bailed out of positions that favored further weakening in the buck. It also soared 2% against the euro, while also climbing dramatically against its other key rivals. The greenback is nearly five yen higher from where it traded a month ago when the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought dollars aggressively to keep the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling below Y105.  \par \par \uc2 Contrary to conventional economic theory, common wisdom among fixed income professionals in recent months has been that the dollar's loss is the bond market's gain.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's finance ministry, with its nearly limitless arsenal of cash, has bought a record amount of dollars to stop the yen from strengthening and derailing its export-led recovery. Much of those \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proceeds have been funneled back into the U.S. Treasurys. Last month, bond market analysts believe Japan accounted for the lion's share of the $25 billion in new Treasury issuance purchased by indirect bidders in the U.S. government's $56 billion quarterly refunding auctions.  \par \par \uc2 "Obviously there's been an effect from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the Treasury market, said Gemma Wright, bond market strategist at Barclays Capital in New York. "There's definitely a concern that if the dollar starts to stabilize in here, there will be less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," and therefore less investment in Treasurys, she said.  \par \par \uc2 But looking at changes in the benchmark 10-year note on Tuesday and those in the dollar against the yen, it appears a weaker dollar has a lot more sway than a stronger one. Part of this is due to a pervasive belief that the Japanese government will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no matter what the dollar level, in an attempt to make the most of what could prove to be only a respite from a broader dollar downtrend.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the view is that in the near term, despite the improvement in the dollar, you are going to continue to see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars put into the Treasury market," said J.P. Marra, head of U.S. government bond trading at Lehman Brothers in New York.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market traders are convinced Japan's monetary authorities jumped into the fray in overnight trade Tuesday, buying over $1 billion, according to some estimates. "In the longer term, obviously, if the dollar continues to move higher, the market will begin to price in less aggressive Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Marra added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Skepticism Creeps In \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While much has been made of the correlation between moves in the dollar and Treasury yield levels in recent weeks, the 10-year note is yielding less now than it was a month ago when Japanese monetary authorities fought hard to keep the dollar afloat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the beginning of last month, the 10-year note fetched 4.10%, about five basis points lower than current levels, while the dollar threatened to break below Y105.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'm not sure this is all dollar-related," said Brian Edmonds, head of U.S. government debt trading at Banc of America Securities in New York, referring to weakness in Treasurys Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are looking for something to talk about," he said, noting that there is now a short leash on Treasury price movements, no matter what the dollar does. That is likely to keep volatility in check at least until Friday, when the Labor Department releases the latest monthly employment statistics.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street economists expect February nonfarm payrolls to post a 125,000 gain versus the 112,000 increase reported in the previous month. While a stronger number is not likely to change much in the bond market, a surprisingly weaker one could push the 10-year yield below 3.92%, the bottom end of its current range, since it promises to deepen skepticism on the sustainability of growth in an economy incapable of creating new jobs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Right now, there is a tug-of-war between foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows and selling pressure from domestic investors who continue to bet that yields will move higher sooner rather than later, traders say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weighing in once again on the subject on Tuesday, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan reaffirmed his view that the massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Asian central banks isn't sustainable. He has stated several times in recent weeks that a withdrawal from U.S. markets by these investors wouldn't have a dramatic impact.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane has covered U.S. credit markets for over three years.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122;  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-03-04 2207GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040302e032000vp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
