\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For March 2  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japan's monetary authorities have scored a notable victory in their fight to brake the yen's rise, as data show speculative sentiment has swung decisively against the yen for the first time in six months. But the Finance Ministry may be a victim of its own success: the shift in futures-market sentiment may actually make it harder for the ministry, through the Bank of Japan, to keep local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains in check. "In the short term, the BOJ can no longer rely on extremely extended short dollar-yen positioning to enhance the effectiveness of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Westpac senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Richard Franulovich. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: BOJ's Success Vs Yen Makes \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Harder" ran at 0249 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian retail sales rebounded in January, showing the domestic economy retains fair momentum and helping to keep alive the possibility of a rate hike in coming months. ("=UPDATE: Australia Sales Rebound, Growth Forecasts Shaved" ran at 0510 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese government bond prices Tuesday slumped to their lowest levels in over a month after a Y1.9 trillion auction of 10-year JGBs that few wanted to buy. ("=UPDATE: JGBs Slump As Poor Auction Signals Rally Is Over" ran at 0902 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: The Bank of Japan's decision last week to consider lending government bonds to dealers to improve liquidity may not be the last move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb premature surges in long-term interest rates. (=BOJ WATCH may run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks likely to issue more government debt than expected this year, keeping bond yields high despite the new prime minister's attempts to reduce the budget deficit. Bond traders had been expecting Malaysia to cut domestic bond sales by nearly 17% this year as the government trimmed the deficit. But that was before Bank Negara Malaysia last month published its auction calendar for this year, which shows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is instead stepping up the frequency of bond sales - suggesting an increase in the amount sold. ("=ASIA DEBT: Malaysia Bonds To Stay Weak On Hefty Supply" ran at 0123 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's monetary base in February grew 16.2% on year, outstripping January's 13.6% growth, in the wake of the Bank of Japan's Jan. 20 move to ease monetary policy further, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed Tuesday. ("Japan Feb Monetary Base Growth 16.2% On Year Vs Jan 13.6%" ran at 0016 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's purchases of shares from commercial banks picked up pace slightly in the nine days through Feb. 29, indicating some demand by banks to unload shares ahead of the March 31 fiscal year end. ("BOJ Share Holdings Y1.922T At Feb 29 Vs Y1.917T Feb 20" ran at 0226 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Offshore issuance of New Zealand dollar-denominated bonds boomed in February, and more such bonds are on the way. Three New Zealand dollar uridashis - bonds which are sold to retail customers in Japan and denominated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than yen - are in the process of being launched or due to be launched this month. One is expected to be the largest-ever New Zealand dollar uridashi. ("=Offshore NZD Bond Issues Boom As Huge Uridashi Awaited" ran at 0808 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Three Chinese brokerages will be permitted to sell corporate bonds, the first approvals for securities firms to issue corporate debt in the country, major financial dailies report. The firms are Citic Securities, Haitong Securities and Great Wall Securities. ("China Brokers Citic, Haitong, Great Wall To Sell Bonds" ran at 2351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Shanghai Double Happiness Yacht Co. barely makes a difference in the bottom line of its parent company, a Chinese sporting goods manufacturing giant whose "DHS" brand is a household name to the millions of table tennis fans in China. But the four-year-old yacht-making arm of Shanghai Double Happiness (Group) Inc. is ambitious.("=CHINA WATCH: China Ping Pong Mfg Taps Taiwan Yacht Skill" ran at 0427 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Overseas visitors to New Zealand in January surged from a year earlier as the tourism sector continued to benefit from the international publicity created by The Lord of the Rings film trilogy. But the average length of stay fell as the strong local dollar is making the country a more expensive destination. ("DATA SNAP: NZ Tourism Strong In Jan; Net Migration Jumps" ran at 2149 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's consumer prices inched higher in February as rising costs of raw materials outweighed feeble personal spending. But analysts said there was no sign of inflationary pressures that would force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates. ("DATA VIEW: S Korea Feb CPI Edges Up; Spending Still Weak" ran at 0327 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's market for nonperforming loan transactions is expected to lose steam after the island's biggest-ever NPL sale takes place later this month, says Clifford So, an assistant vice president at REPro International Inc. Chung Shing Bank is set to offer NT$52.4 billion in NPLs at the end of March, marking the highlight of the market this year, So said. ("=INTERVIEW: Taiwan NPL Mkt To Lose Steam After Huge Deal" ran at 0546 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Reflecting the challenges in cleaning up the state bank sector's massive nonperforming loan burdens, China Great Wall Asset Management Corp.'s disposals of bad assets from state-owned Agricultural Bank of China will slow significantly over the next three years, state media reported. (story will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday said it had uncovered irregularities in the international balance of payment reports of several unnamed domestic banks. (story will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Hanaro Telecom said Tuesday it is planning to issue $600 million worth of U.S. dollar-denominated bonds this year. ("S Korea Hanaro Telecom Plans To Issue $600M Bonds In '04" ran at 0206 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The Reserve Bank of New Zealand will raise the Official Cash Rate for the second and final time this year at its March 11 policy statement, according to a majority of forecasters in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. ("=UPDATE: Consensus Of NZ Forecasters Picks Mar Rate Hike" ran at 2256 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rose slightly against the yen in Asia Tuesday amid rumors that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities covertly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the dollar. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Up Vs Yen On Suspected MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0548 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Airports of Thailand raised 17.33 billion baht from its initial public offering after Tuesday setting the offer price at the top end of an indicative range amid strong investor demand. AOT sold 412.57 million shares at THB42 a share. Airports of Thailand is the first state enterprise to be listed on the local bourse since late 2001, when state-owned PTT PCL offered shares to the public. (UPDATE will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares of steelmaker China Oriental Group closed 24% higher at HK$3.40 on their first day of trading on Hong Kong's main board, compared with their IPO price of HK$2.75. (UPDATE will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: The Australian government Tuesday reacted cautiously to court action by a small U.S. petroleum company that is seeking US$10.5 billion in damages over its alleged oil and gas rights in the Timor Sea. The suit, filed by Oceanic Exploration in U.S. District Court in Washington D.C., is the latest twist in a long-running feud over ownership of billions of dollars worth of oil and gas reserves located in waters to the north of Australia. ("=UPDATE: Australian Govt Cautious On East Timor Oil Claim" ran at 0821 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Rail operator MTR Corp. said its 2003 net profit surged to HK$4.45 billion from HK$3.58 billion in 2002, mainly as a result of a large sale of prime real estate. ("HK MTR Corp Full-Year Net Profit HK$4.45 Bln On Ppty Sale" ran at 0820 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toyota Motor says it will spend EUR75 million to beef up its European research and development \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next five years as part of an effort to design cars more suited to the European market. ("Toyota To Spend EUR75 Mln To Set Up New R&D Ops In Europe" ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Softbank has received about $1.135 billion in fresh loans from Citibank, as the Softbank group scrambles to deal with a case of massive customer personal data leakage of its "Yahoo! BB" broadband service. ("Softbank Gets $1.135 Bln Citibank Loan For Broadband Ops" ran at 2353 GMT; note "CORRECT: Softbank Loan From Citibank US, Not Japan, Unit" that ran at 0010 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Temasek Holdings, the Singapore government's investment arm, Tuesday said it has bought a 5% stake in Telekom Malaysia. The deal marks a significant change in mindset for Malaysia and could pave the way for similar deals between the two countries. ("=UPDATE: Singapore Temasek Buys 5% Of Telekom Malaysia" ran at 0710 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra told the Cabinet Tuesday he wants the share swap deal between state-owned Electricity Generating Authority of Thailand and coal mining and power generating firm Banpu PCL to be shelved, the chief government spokesman said. The deal was postponed earlier last week due to resistance from EGAT's labor union and unresolved tax and stock regulation issues. (story will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A group led by flag carrier Air China will become the controlling shareholder of regional airline Shandong Airlines Co., the northeastern China-based company said Tuesday. ("REPEAT&CORRECT:Air China Takes 48% Of Shandong Air Parent" ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: State-run Korea Deposit Insurance Corp., or KDIC, plans to sell its entire 21.6% stake in Hana Bank by the end of May, said an official at KDIC Tuesday. ("S Korea Govt To Sell 21.6% Hana Bank Stake By End-May" ran at 0123 GMT) [ 02-03-04 0911GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Consumer electronics maker TCL International said Tuesday its full-year net profit rose 12% to HK$641.8 million from HK$573.7 million. ("HK TCL International FY Net Up 12% To HK$641.8 Mln" ran at 0629 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean auto sales in February rose 18% on year as exports continued to be robust, but domestic sales remained sluggish with no signs of arecovery in the near term, data released Tuesday by the country's five auto makers showed. (UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: APN News & Media Ltd., a company with extensive radio and newspaper assets in Australasia, reported Tuesday a 15% rise in 2003 net profit, as it reaped the rewards of improved ratings, circulation figures and advertising conditions. ("=UPDATE: Australia's APN News & Media FY Pft Jumps 15%" ran at 0349 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Uni-President Enterprises Corp., Taiwan's largest food company, has bought a 31.25% stake in dairy product maker Kuang Chuan Dairy Co., as part of its efforts to become Asia's largest food conglomerate. ("Taiwan's Uni-President Takes 31.25% Stake In Kuang Chuan" ran at 0510 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Dah Sing Financial said Tuesday its 2003 net profit jumped 16.5% as its operating profit rose. The bank's net profit last year rose to HK$992.6 million from HK$852.3 million the previous year. ("HK Dah Sing Financial Full-Year Net Up 16.5% At HK$992.6M" ran at 0634 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Asia-Pacific airline executives can expect to be wined and dined by the world's two biggest aircraft manufacturers, Boeing and Airbus, with the region's aviation sector expected to outpace the rest of the world over the next two decades. ("=INTERVIEW:Asia-Pacific Aviation Mkt Set To Outpace World" ran at 0419 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Kia Motors, a unit of South Korea's biggest carmaker Hyundai Motor, said it has chosen Zilina in Slovakia to build the company's European production base. ("=UPDATE: S Korea Kia Picks Slovakia For Euro Plant Site" ran at 0728 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Stephen Tindall, acting managing director of retailer The Warehouse Group, has set a deadline for its Australian stores to make a profit or the company will pursue "a different strategy." ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Warehouse Sets Deadline For Australian Ops" ran at 0222 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Port of Tauranga expects earnings in its second half to be stronger than the first half, helped by record coal import volumes and new business from P&O Nedlloyd's express service to South East Asia, Chief Executive Jon Mayson said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Tauranga Port Sees 2H Net Stronger Than 1H" ran at 0109 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Fast Retailing said its same-store sales in February climbed 18.4% on year, marking the third straight month of rise, and an encouraging sign for the Uniqlo casual-clothing chain. ("Fast Retailing February Same-Store Sales Up 18.4% On Year" ran at 0623 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Xinjiang Guanghui LNG Development Co., a privately owned company in northwestern China, will begin supplying liquefied natural gas to power producers in Guangdong province in April, three years ahead of government-owned China National Offshore Oil Corp., the company's marketing manager said. ("=INTERVIEW: Upstart To Vie With CNOOC For S China LNG Mkt" ran at 0526 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: A bouyant commodity price cycle underpinned by a robust global economy could peak in 2004, the government's Australian Bureau of Agricultural and Resource Economics said Tuesday. The strong global economy will contribute to a recovery in the value of commodity exports from Australia next fiscal year ending June 2005, ending several years of declining exports, it said. ("=UPDATE:Australian Forecaster Says Commod Cycle Near Peak" ran at 0838 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Severe winds from Cyclone Monty have delayed a planned production restart at Exxon Mobil's Wandoo oil field off the coast of Western Australia state. ("Exxon Mobil Delays Australian Wandoo Oil Output Restart" ran at 0148 GMT) [ 02-03-04 0911GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040302e032000cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict