\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Greenspan Says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Is Risky  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Rebello and Deborah Lagomarsino  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,021 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON(Dow Jones)--Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned Tuesday that Japan's efforts to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising against the U.S. dollar has resulted in an "awesome" accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that could soon become "problematic" for the world's second-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking to a group of economists in New York, Greenspan offered an unusually critical assessment of Japan's exchange-rate policies. Since the start of 2002, he said, Japanese authorities have engaged in extraordinarily large purchases of U.S. dollar assets to hold down the value of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Even so, he said, "the yen appears to be elevated relative to the dollar," partly because average Japanese investors show an unusually intense preference for yen-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said Japanese "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has "influenced the market value of the yen," although the magnitude of the effect is "difficult to judge." In any case, he said, the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unsustainable. "It must be presumed that the rate of accumulation of dollar assets by the Japanese government will have to slow at some point and eventually cease," he said in prepared remarks to the Economic Club of New York.  \par \par \uc2 For now, Greenspan said, the purchases are consistent with Japan's anti-deflationary monetary policies. "In time, however, as the present deflationary situation abates, the monetary consequences of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become problematic," Greenspan said. "The current performance of the Japanese economy suggests that we are getting closer to the point where continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the present scale will no longer meet the monetary-policy needs of Japan."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan's comments appeared to shift attention from China's exchange-rate policies to those of Japan, which U.S. officials have not criticized for years. Over the last year, U.S. lawmakers, prodded by manufacturers, have complained bitterly that China's fixed exchange rate system has brought a tide of inexpensive imports into the U.S. - at the cost of U.S. workers. The Bush administration has pressed China to adopt a more "flexible" system. Greenspan, however, suggested that China should change its system gradually.  \par \par \uc2 Since the first quarter of 2002, the dollar has fallen 30% against the European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but just 17% against the Japanese yen and not at all against the Chinese yuan. Greenspan said the dollar's smaller decline against the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflected "the extraordinary purchases by Asian central banks and governments," mainly Japan and China. Those purchases have totaled almost $240 billion, he said. As a result, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surpassed $650 billion in December while China's hit $420 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said the yen's "elevated" value versus the U.S. dollar also reflected an unusually intense preference among average Japanese investors for Japanese assets. "Nowhere else in the world will investors voluntarily purchase 10-year government obligations with an interest rate of 1% or less, especially given a rate of increase in the outstanding supply of government debt that has generally been running at 9% over the past year," he said. "Not surprisingly, very few Japanese government bonds are held outside of Japan."  \par \par \uc2 "The degree of domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bias in Japan...may thus have contributed to a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate for the yen that appears to be elevated relative to the dollar and possibly other internationally traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well," Greenspan said. "Of course this preference for yen assets, while a persistent influence on the value of the yen, has at times been overwhelmed by other factors."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said China's exchange-rate system, which pegs the Chinese yuan to the dollar at a rate of roughly eight to one, also will eventually need to be modified. "Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars, unless offset, threaten an excess of so-called high-powered money expansion and a consequent overheating of the Chinese economy," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Eventually, Chinese authorities will face a stark choice, Greenspan said: reduce purchases of U.S. dollars or deal with "an overheated economy with the associated economic instabilities." A reduction of the dollar purchases, Greenspan said, "presumably would allow the renminbi, at least temporarily, to appreciate against the dollar." The renminbi is another name for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Greenspan said he disagreed with the views of some commentators that Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has placed "upward pressure on the euro." Instead, he said, "a more likely possibility is that the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had little effect on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the trade-weighted average of the dollar is, thus, somewhat elevated relative to the rate that would have prevailed absent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 "With fewer dollar assets in private hands, the natural inclination to rebalance portfolios will tend to buoy the dollar even against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are not used in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro," Greenspan said. "These transactions raise the dollar against, for example, the yen, lower the yen against the euro, and lower the euro against the dollar. The strength of the euro against the dollar thus appears to be the consequence of forces unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan also played down fears that a slowdown in Asian purchases of U.S. assets - mainly Treasury bonds - will spur an increase in bond interest rates. "Some have argued that purchases of U.S. Treasurys by Asian officials are holding down interest rates on these instruments, and therefore U.S. interest rates are likely to rise as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian monetary authorities slows, ceases, or even turns to net sales," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "While there are obvious reasons to be concerned about such an outcome, the effect of a reduction in the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or even net sales, on U.S. financial markets would likely be small," Greenspan said. That's because Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are "heavily concentrated in short-term maturities" and the overall market for short-term dollar assets is "huge relative to the size" of Asian reserves, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Joseph Rebello, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9279; joseph.rebello@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-02-04 1358ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4216  \par \par \uc2 Dokumen