\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Sell Off On Dlr Strength; 10-Yr Yield At 4.05%  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Grainne McCarthy  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 18:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Continued uncertainty about the direction of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates, coupled with the breach of key technical levels, has sent the dollar soaring against its principal rivals Tuesday, hitting U.S. stocks and bonds in the process.  \par \par \uc2 Having already started the week on a positive note, the dollar catapulted higher against the euro after shattering key single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support around $1.2350 and then $1.2330, analysts said. Around midday in New York, the euro was trading at session lows of $1.2208, sharply down from its high for the day at $1.2468 and over two cents down from $1.2445 late Monday in New York, to hit the lowest level since December last year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y110.18, up more than a full yen from Y109.05 late Monday. It traded as high as Y110.49, its highest level since the first week of November 2003, with dealers also citing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  as instrumental in pushing the dollar higher against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also surged to a new three-month peak against the Swiss franc at CHF1.2975, up sharply from CHF1.2685, while sterling traded at $1.8374 after tumbling to a one-month low at $1.8355 from $1.8670 late Monday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 While the veracity of the move in the dollar has been fueled by technical factors, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is also reflecting some jitters heading into Thursday's ECB rate-setting meeting, and that's despite consensus expectations for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold its key interest rate steady at 2%.  \par \par \uc2 "No one really wanted to be long euros before the ECB to begin with," said Alan Ruskin, research director at 4Cast financial consultancy in New York. "Basically there is nothing to be gained from it."  \par \par \uc2 For the eighth straight month, ECB watchers polled by Dow Jones Newswires were unanimous in saying they don't expect any move on rates. Additionally, the 28 respondents expect euro-zone rates to hold steady through June, with no shift in its monetary policy bias.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some investors in the market evidently won't believe that until they see it and argue that any hint of an upcoming rate cut would undermine some of the yield advantage of holding the euro relative to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is moving to factor in the possibility of a strong February employment report, set for release Friday. Some believe signs of strength in the labor market could bring about a Fed rate hike sooner than many anticipate.  \par \par \uc2 "We had a major shakedown in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. "It's a cocktail of factors as people square up ahead of the ECB meeting and the U.S. employment report."  \par \par \uc2 In the Treasurys market, while intuitively dollar strength should be beneficial, prices are reacting to the possibility that sustained dollar strength will remove a key source of support from the market. Asian governments, led by Japan, have been major buyers of Treasurys for months as they park dollars bought via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. government bonds. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a massive force in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets as it seeks to limit the impact of a strong yen on its export-led economy.  \par \par \uc2 "There's definitely a concern that if the dollar starts to stabilize in here, there will be less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and therefore less investment in Treasurys by the Bank of Japan, said Gemma Wright, bond market strategist at Barclays Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Those fears have pushed the benchmark 10-year yield above the key 4% threshold, while weighing on prices - which move inversely to yields - across the board. U.S. equities are also under water as a weaker dollar has handed a key competitive advantage to U.S. corporations.  \par \par \uc2 While the selloff was sharp Tuesday, the 10-year note hasn't yet broken out of the range in which it's been for weeks, as most bond market traders believe the Fed will keep rates on hold until after the Presidential election in November.  \par \par \uc2 Early afternoon, the Dow Jones Industrial Average is down 0.6%, while the S&P 500 Compositive is down 0.4%.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:20 p.m. EST, the 10-year Treasury note stood at 99 19/32, down 15/32 to yield 4.05%, 5.8 basis points higher from where it traded late Monday. The 30-year bond was off 27/32 to 106 28/32, yielding 4.91%, up 5.0 basis ponts.  \par \par \uc2 -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Agnes T. Crane and Robert Flint contributed to this article) [ 03-02-04 1233ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040302e03200029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
