\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: HSBC Earns, US ISM Data Eyed  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 US SUMMARY: US Stocks Mixed; Treasurys Post Gains  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA         10538.92    gain  3.78    up 0.1% \par NASDAQ        2029.82    down  2.75    dn 0.1% \par S&P 500       1144.94    unchanged \par NASDAQ Future 1479.00    gain  7.50    up 0.5% \par S&P Future    1148.80    gain  4.20    up 0.4% \par 10-Yr U.S. Treasury: 100, up 12/32 to yield 4.00%   \f28 \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained a touch on the final trading day of February, as United Technologies and Boeing rebounded and U.S. economic growth defied expectations.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Leading the blue-chip advance, United Technologies rose 2.5% and Boeing climbed 2.2%, retracing part of a slide that began earlier in the week when the U.S. Army said it would abandon the Comanche helicopter program.  \par \par \uc2 Stock indexes got off to a strong start on news the U.S. economy grew at a faster pace than expected in the fourth quarter, but gains withered by mid-session amid light trading volume.  \par \par \uc2 All in all, analysts said investors are waiting for a new catalyst to determine the direction of the market. Next Friday's unemployment report for February could provide the impetus stocks lack, as could the advent of earnings pre-announcement season in March.  \par \par \uc2 "The reason people are being standoff-ish is that the bar has been raised for the next quarter," said James Park, senior vice president of equities trading at Brean Murray. "The market has been discounting higher expectations going forward."  \par \par \uc2 There were some notable decliners on the Nasdaq, including Genzyme. Shares fell 5.3% after the biotechnology firm agreed to buy small-cap Ilex Oncology for $1 billion in stock. Oracle shed 3.1% on news antitrust enforcers filed suit to block Oracle's $9.4 billion hostile bid for rival software maker PeopleSoft.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: The U.S. Treasury market made good gains Friday, but market participants said the rise had little to do with the day's economic data and was instead the result of more mundane technical factors.  \par \par \uc2 "People are just looking at month-end extensions" and hedging as the reason why Treasurys are up Friday, said Peter Cordrey, managing director with Prudential Investment Management in Newark, N.J.  \par \par \uc2 "It's technical," Cordrey said, as he noted that "the market seems to be in balance" in terms of market participants' positions. He noted that the current 10-year note yield sits squarely between the 4.12% and 3.87% marks that have defined the most recent trading range.  \par \par \uc2 Mark Mahoney, strategist with UBS in Stamford, Conn., said "the market has figured out you don't have the pieces in place" to drive prices into some new range, given the absence of surprises on the growth or inflation front.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also continued to play a role in the Treasurys market, as the weak dollar continued to exert downward pressure on yields.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to buy massive amounts of dollars in February. The BOJ spent the equivalent of $31.5 billion supporting the dollar, which is presumably recycled back into U.S. fixed-income securities.  \par \par \uc2 ASIAN SUMMARY: Nikkei Surges To 20-Mo High, Bonds Soft  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par USD-Yen:      109.35     gain 0.23      up 0.21% \par AUD-USD:      0.7743     gain 0.0008    up 0.10% \par Hang Seng:    13970.19   gain 63.16     up 0.45% \par Nikkei 225:   11287.80   gain 245.88    up 2.23% \par 10-yr JGB: The yield is up 3.0 basis points at 1.255%   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks climbed nearly 2.5% to a 20-month high, as investors snapped up technology issues and blue chip exporters in response to the U.S. dollar's rise against the Japanese yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: On the stock market, technology and other blue chips - such as electronics giant Sony and automaker Toyota Motor - posted gains on the dollar's recent rebound against the yen and signs that Japan's economy is recovering.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rising shares lifted the Nikkei to its highest level since the blue-chip index traded above 11,300 in mid-June 2002.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign investors' buying has increased, which probably is one key factor for the rise of the Nikkei index," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are buying because the strong trend of GDP growth seems to be continuing in Japan from the last quarter with industrial output and household spending rising in January," said Sato.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Japanese government bond prices fell Monday on continued strength in share prices as well as the yen's slide against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday's 10-year auction is also weighing on cash bond prices, with the new coupon likely to be set at 1.3%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks To Open Higher, Bonds Seen Down  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Euro-USD      1.2491     gain 0.0001    up 0.01% \par Stlg-USD      1.8680     gain 0.0004    up 0.02% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--European stocks should open higher amid strong gains in Asian markets, while bonds should start lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spreadbettor IG Index is calling the FTSE 100 up 16 points at 4508, with both the DAX and CAC seen firmer, up 12 and 10 points respectively.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European stocks were mostly higher Friday as exporters got a boost from the euro's retreat against the dollar and U.S. economic data helped ease investor fears about the pace of economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger dollar provided a fillip for companies that generate a large proportion of sales in the U.S  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. equities underperformed as the FTSE 100 index came under heavy selling pressure toward the close. Traders said the selling was due to end-of-month book squaring.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Shaw, economist at Investec, said the latest U.S. data show an unbroken picture of strong economic growth. He added that Monday's U.S. Institute of Supply Management data will be key to maintaining market momentum. Cyclical stocks and export-dependent companies saw most of the gains Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors' attention will focus on expected strong earnings from Europe's biggest bank, HSBC Holdings. Media giant Pearson and luxury automaker Porsche will also update the market with earnings announcements.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: European government bonds should open softer Monday on the continued rise in global equities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bonds ended the trading week on a positive note Friday, with lower-than-expected euro-zone inflation data reigniting speculation that a rate cut from the ECB may be just round the corner.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said the data were a clear indication that inflationary pressures were easing, giving the ECB room to cut interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The dollar is expected to continue choppy trading against its major crosses this week amid growing uncertainty about whether the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will act to counter euro strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may cut interest rates at its monetary policy meeting Thursday - or even \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - has been behind much of the dollar's rise over the past week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most analysts don't expect the ECB to cut rates at the coming meeting, though they will be paying close attention to the subsequent press conference by ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet for signs of any shift in bias.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until the market gets the result of that meeting and the U.S. February payroll numbers Friday, volatile market conditions will probably continue, say market participants.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a lot of uncertainty about the way the price action evolved (last) week, and people are wary about where the Federal Reserve and the ECB are going," says Daniel Katzive, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS in Stamford, Conn. "But ultimately, I think this is a correction, and once positions are cleared out, the dollar can resume its downtrend."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CALENDAR: Monday, Mar. 1:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT/EST  Country Indicator/Event \par  \par 0830/0330  ITA  2003 GDP and Public Administration Debt \par 0850/0350  FRA  Feb Manufacturing PMI \par 0855/0355  GER  Feb Manufacturing PMI \par 0930/0430  UK   Feb Manufacturing PMI \par 0930/0430  UK   Jan Consumer Lending \par 1100/0600  FRA  Feb New Vehicle Registrations \par 1330/0830  US   Jan Personal Income \par 1330/0830  US   Jan Personal Spending \par 1400/0900  US   Treasury Under Secy Roseboro speaks in Washington \par 1400/0900  FRA  Weekly T-bill Auction results \par 1430/0930  GER  ECB calls for bids on main refi ops \par 1430/0930  US   Fed Gov Olson speaks in Washington \par 1500/1000  US   Jan Construction Spending \par 1500/1000  US   Feb ISM Manufacturing Index \par 1630/1130  ITA  Treasury expected to release SSBR \par 2330/1830  JPN  10-year Government Bond Auction \par 2350/1850  JPN  Feb Monetary Base \par 2350/1850  JPN  Mar Money Market Forecast   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Paul Larkins; Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207-842-9319; paul.larkins@dowjones.com [ 01-03-04 0600GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HSBC Holdings (HBC): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, analysts): GBP13.2B (GBP9.7B) \par Note: Analysts said U.S. dollar weakness could make non-dollar investors disappointed when they convert the earnings and dividends into euros or pounds, but there won't be major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects for the company as a whole. About a third of HSBC's earnings is earned in pounds and euros that then must be converted to dollars, so there will be some inflation. \par  \par Pearson (PSO): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit (JCF, 9 analysts): GBP414.15M (GBP399M) \par Consensus sales: GBP3.99B (GBP4.32B) \par Note: Investors will seek signs of improved outlook at flagship Financial Times newspaper, which has been hammered by advertising slump; progress report on education, which is heavily weighted to 2H; and impact of USD weakness on revenues. \par  \par Aggregate Industries (AGG.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit after goodwill amortization (Company, 12 analysts): GBP139.7M (GBP134.5M) \par Note:  US weather, weak dollar seen impacting latest year, while prospects for US recovery and continued UK government spending eyed for impact on current year. One analyst says focus may also be on whether recent US aggregate acquisitions can generate returns that cover cost of capital at a time when interest rates are set to rise. \par  \par Aggreko (AGK.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit (Company, 10 analysts): GBP40M (GBP55.1M) \par Forecast range: GBP37M - GBP40.2M \par Note:  Main event is result of strategic review by new CEO Rupert Soames. Brewin Dolphin analyst Iain Armstrong says company needs to try to get a business model where there's greater certainty on earnings and revenue." Consolidating network of depots worldwide might boost efficiency, and better sales efforts might also help. \par  \par Persimmon (PSN.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit before exceptional items and goodwill (Company, 18 analysts): GBP343.3M (GBP267.6M) \par Forecast range: GBP xx.x M - GBP xx.x M \par Consensus sales: GBP x.xx B (GBP x.xx B) \par Note: Analysts looking at prospects for growth through acquisition, as company is only one of big 4 homebuilders with cash and inclination. One analyst says, "it's hard to get organic growth" as shown by industry peer George Wimpey (WMPY.LN) this week, and with interest rates rising and volume growth restricted, wonders how much house price gains can continue to support margin expansion. \par  \par GKN (GKN.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit before goodwill amortization and exceptional items (Company, 15 analysts): GBP243M (GBP267M) \par Note:  Earnings to reflect reduction in North American and European automotive production in 2H. Net figure to include GBP90M impairment charge for Powder Metallurgy business and GBP60M profit on disposal of Alvis (ALV.LN) \par  \par Hammerson (HMSO.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit (Company, 6 analysts): GBP84.2M (GBP85.6M) \par Note: Adjusted EPS seen at 28.34p vs 29.2p, dividend seen 16.7p vs 15.8p. Deutsche Bank sees results "reflecting the continued resilience of the retail portfolio" and is also looking for City letting news and any Paris stock exchange listing plans. \par  \par Inchcape (INCH.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit before exceptional items (Company, 7 analysts): GBP133.7M (GBP112.1M) \par Note:  Analysts say results may be ahead of estimates despite December trading statement. "Inchcape's results have routinely beat expectations," says one analyst. Looking to see where company will deploy growing cash pile, with GBP30M VAT exceptional gain expected. \par  \par Exel (EXL.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax profit before items (Company, 15 analysts): GBP143.1M (GBP135.3M) \par Note: Results will be hit by weaker dollar translation effects, says CSFB, but expects this to become less significant in 2004. Still plenty of growth prospects for the company and well positioned to win further market share. \par  \par Ferrovial (FER.MC): FY Earnings \par Consensus net profit (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR280M (EUR456M) \par Note: EBIT +21% to EUR588M vs EUR485M, sales +18% to EUR5.9B vs EUR5.0B on better performance in service division after incorporation of Amey, Cespa. Eyes on 04 outlook, 4Q margins. Shares +0.1% at EUR29.80. \par  \par Close Brothers Group (CBG.LN): 1H Earnings \par Pretax profit before items (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP50.4M (GBP39.7M) \par Note: Focus will be on growth in all businesses driven by market recovery but especially Winterflood market-making \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par OTHER SCHEDULED EVENTS: \par  \par Porsche  (POR3.XE): 1H Earnings \par  \par British Polythene Industries (BPI.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Wolfson Microelectronics (WLF.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Vitec (VTC.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Statpro Group (SOC.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par British Vita (BVIT.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Systems Union Group (SUG.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Management Consulting (MMC.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Imprint Search & Select (IMP.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par OHL (OHL.MC): FY Earnings \par  \par CCF (CCFH): FY Earnings \par  \par Sacyr-Valleheromoso (SYV.MC): FY Earnings \par  \par Thiel Logistik (TGH.XE): FY Earnings press conference, Frankfurt \par  \par KGHM Polska Miedz (KGH.WA): 4Q/FY Consol Earnings \par  \par Kredyt Bank (KRB.WA): 4Q Earnings \par  \par ING Bank Slaski (BSK.WA): 4Q Earnings \par  \par Techem (TNH.XE): 1Q Earnings \par  \par Benchmark Group (BMK.LN): 1H Earni