\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Rally May Be Facing Some Obstacles  \b0\par\par\uc2 974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar's rally in recent weeks from lows against its major rivals has been impressive, but it may be grinding to a halt as market participants reconsider two of its principal drivers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A huge shift in the holdings of short-term, or speculative investors has left the market much more balanced and less predisposed to buy dollars. Meanwhile, hopes that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might cut interest rates this week on the back of soft inflation data last week are quickly fading, suggesting that the euro may edge back up towards its all-time highs, economists reckon.  \par \par \uc2 "This is about as good as it gets for the dollar," said David Durrant, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Julius Baer in New York. "You'll probably come out of the week with a less bullish feel than you went into it with."  \par \par \uc2 Durrant expects the euro to trade in a range of $1.2400-$1.2900 in the coming weeks. On Monday afternoon, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at $1.2438, down from $1.2495 late Friday in New York and within a cent of the $1.2375 one-month low plumbed on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide - which also pulled down the dollar's other major counterparts - followed aggressive selling after the latest snapshot of inflation in the euro zone fueled expectations that the ECB will lower its benchmark refinancing rate this week from 2%.  \par \par \uc2 The flash estimate for February's consumer price index came in at 1.6%, down from 1.9% in January and significantly below the ECB's target of "close to, but below" 2% annual inflation. A rate cut would modestly reduce the euro's yield allure against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and encourage investors seeking higher returns to pull out of euro-denominated assets and park their cash elsewhere.  \par \par \uc2 Judging by the recent commentary from ECB officials however, it looks increasingly likely that there will be no change in rates on Thursday. ECB policy-makers have argued that interest rates are "appropriate" at these levels and that a rate cut would do little in any case to influence the euro's exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Economists reckon the ECB will need at least a couple of months of soft inflation numbers and below-forecast confidence and purchasing managers' index data to trigger a cu. And besides, a euro closer to $1.20 than $1.30 removes some of the urgency becasue it lessens the pressure on exporters from a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they add.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Risk Again Reversed Against The Dollar \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Rothfield, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of America in San Francisco, says the dollar's gains in recent weeks represent a healthy correction. But pointing to the correlation between its previous couple of corrections higher and the picture currently painted by risk reversals, it looks like it's fizzling out, he says.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk reversals measure the premium investors are willing to pay for holding "call" or "put" options on a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Call options are effectively bets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will strengthen and puts that it will fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having favored euro calls and dollar puts for several months, the one-month options market is now broadly neutral on options in the euro against the dollar. This is a sign investors in this market are ready to buy euros and sell dollars again, Rothfield reckons.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk reversals aren't the only short-term market indicator suggesting the dollar's correction may be losing momentum.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest International Monetary Market data from the Chicago futures exchange show \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speculators dramatically reduced their bets against the dollar in the week to Feb. 24, particularly against the yen. This largely explains the dollar's recent climb against the yen, which culminated in a three-month high of Y109.67 Friday, from around Y105.65 on Feb. 17, analysts say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data, which measure the positioning of speculative investors, showed a huge reversal to a net long yen position of 12,181 contracts worth around $1.4 billion from a net short yen position of 55,198 contracts worth around $6.3 billion the week before. This is the first time in seven months that these speculative accounts' portfolios were tilted against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Part of the reason behind this startling turnaround is the persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which has frustrated efforts to push the yen stronger than Y105. The finance ministry sold a massive Y10.5 trillion in the first two months of the year - around half of last year's record total - in trying to keep the yen competitively weak for the country's exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, the net short dollar position against all its major rivals "collapsed" in the most recent week by around $12 billion to just over $6 billion, the smallest since the September meetings of the Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Dubai, noted T.J. Marta, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In combination with a lack of change in fundamental considerations, (this) supports our view that the recent dollar rally was simply short covering and not indicative of further gains in coming weeks," Marta said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Those considerations include low U.S. interest rates and a wide U.S. current account deficit, running at around 5% of gross domestic product.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Despite this large change in speculative positions, the consensus view supports a weaker dollar both against the euro and the yen," wrote Deutsche Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts in a research note.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-01-04 1519ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040301e0310002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
