\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar To Dance To  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Tunes  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Evans  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Februar 2004\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Australia and New Zealand Report\par \uc2 DJAN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The exceptional volatility and ragged price movements that have recently characterized global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are likely to persist this week, with the dollar's fate influenced by a heavy slate of economic data and monetary policy decisions at major central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Depending on how the data and policy announcements turn out, the dollar is seen at risk of a pullback against major rivals like the yen and the euro, though it may first test some key technical resistance areas.  \par \par \uc2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was trading around $1.2495, up from $1.2430 late Thursday in New York, and at Y136.21 versus Y136.30. The dollar was weaker against the yen at Y109.15 from Y109.63 late Thursday, and had declined to CHF1.2632 from CHF1.2675. The pound rose to $1.8649 from $1.8610.  \par \par \uc2 Despite its rally back over the past several weeks from multiyear lows against many widely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar is still seen in the midst of a necessary correction from oversold levels, rather than in the initial stages of a more sustained rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Current market conditions have many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  professionals speaking of a "trading market" in which short-term technical considerations loom large, instead of a "positioning market" in which a more fundamental change in the relative outlook toward the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is underway.  \par \par \uc2 "In the broader picture, we're still in a range-trading environment with some lightening up of long \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposures, rather than there being any significant shift toward a more positive view of the dollar," according to senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Lisa Finstrom of Citigroup Global Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Low Interest Rates Undermine Dollar \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar remains undermined by the view, expressed again over the past week in statements from various Federal Reserve officials, that U.S. interest rates are likely to remain low for the foreseeable future, given the uneven nature of the economic rebound and the evident softness of the labor market and inflation in the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nevertheless, the dollar made some impressive gains last week against the euro, as Europe's single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suffered from speculation that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might ease its key policy rate Thursday in a concession to official concern about the economic effects of a strong euro and also to weak euro-zone inflation readings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's rally several times recently has moved it to the brink of another test of its year-to-date highs around $1.2338 against the euro, and another challenge and possible break of this level is seen possible in advance of Thursday's ECB policy announcement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the extent of any prospective dollar moves will likely be conditioned by fresh perspectives on the U.S. economy flowing from data such as Tuesday's February Institute for Supply Management manufacturing index; the latest Federal Reserve Beige Book on Wednesday; January manufacturing orders on Thursday; and most notably, the pivotal February non-farm payrolls report on Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Johnston, managing director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at RBC Capital Markets in Toronto, said that "the employment situation in the U.S. isn't great for the dollar," and the market will likely need some improvement over January's gain of about 112,000 new jobs to sustain the dollar's better performance recently.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apart from Thursday's ECB decision, key euro-zone economic events include German January retail sales figures along with France's February consumer confidence indicator on Tuesday; and fourth quarter euro-zone gross domestic product figures along with January factory orders and February unemployment data from Germany on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sustains Dollar This Year \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen, the dollar has been sustained this year by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts of Japanese monetary authorities to nurture \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic recovery by slowing the yen's rise. Last week, Japan confirmed that it had sold about Y3.34 trillion, or about $31.5 billion, to support the dollar between Jan. 29 and Feb. 25 - slightly more than half the amount funneled into global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the previous month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts and the dollar's better global tone by last week had lifted it to two-month highs above the Y109.00 mark, but the dollar is seen having limited additional upside, given the underlying support to the yen lent by recent robust Japanese economic data and strong investment inflows in tandem with the rise of Japan's stock market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've seen a lot of evidence in the last several weeks that real money flows are still quite strong and Japanese exporters have a lot of dollars to sell," said financial markets economist Shaun Osborne of Scotia Capital in Toronto. "So we've probably seen about as much as we're going to see in terms of dollar gains, and Y110.00 will probably cap things for now."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other central banks due to make policy announcements this week include the Bank of England, also on Thursday, along with the Bank of Canada and the Reserve Bank of Australia, both on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar could see some gains against sterling and the Australian and Canadian dollars, as most market participants expect neither the Bank of England nor the Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to enhance the lure of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as "high-yield" units by again raising rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Canada, meanwhile, is overwhelmingly expected to lower its key policy rate for a second consecutive time in 2004, further reducing Canada's yield and rate premiums over comparable U.S instruments.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - By Paul Evans; Dow Jones Newswires; (416) 306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.com [ 29-02-04 2200GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1141 4137  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJAN000020040229e02t00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
