\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rally accelerates pre US data,US-German talk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2004\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to fresh one-month highs versus the euro on Friday, extending this week's rally ahead of German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's talks with U.S. President George W. Bush.  \par \par \uc2 A raft of U.S. data, including fourth-quarter growth and consumer sentiment, was also keenly awaited for clues about the U.S. economy and interest rate outlook.  \par \par \uc2 A government source has said Schroeder will make the euro's strength against the dollar a main theme in his meeting with Bush. French and German politicians are concerned about the euro's strength and Schroeder also called this week for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 While the ECB dislikes political pressure, speculation is growing it might consider lowering rates if there is evidence euro strength is putting downward pressure on inflation.  \par \par \uc2 A first estimate of euro zone inflation data showed inflation slid to an annual rate of 1.6 percent in February, below the ECB's target ceiling of 2.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "If we have data coming below target for two to three months then the ECB will genuinely consider cutting rates, but not because of political pressure. The ECB has been saying the current monetary policy is appropriate," said Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 By 1230 GMT, the dollar had risen to $1.2374 per euro, up half a percent from late New York levels. The euro has lost more than five cents from last week's record high near $1.2930.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also lost ground to the yen, dipping one percent to 134.89 yen. Sterling also lost more than one percent against the yen to 201.36 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "All Schroeder is likely to get is a shrug of the shoulder from the U.S., which has clearly said an appropriate level of exchange rates should be set by the market," Derrrick said.  \par \par \uc2 "It will be far better for him and other eurozone officials to speak to Asian officials as Asia has not fully taken part in the adjustment process."  \par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER, US DATA  \par \par \uc2 French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin also criticised euro strength this week and called on the ECB to cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 But ECB council member Nicholas Garganas poured cold water on the idea in a newspaper interview on Friday. He told the Financial Times a strong euro was not stifling the euro zone economy and advised "staying cool" on the exchange rate issue.  \par \par \uc2 "There is always a slight difference between politicians and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the question is how long the ECB can justify holdinf off from cutting interest rates when inflation is moving below the actual target," said Niels Christensen, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale.  \par \par \uc2 From the United States, fourth-quarter gross domestic product, due at 1330 GMT, is expected to show the economy grew by 3.6 percent, down from the previous estimate of 4.0 percent and compared with a blistering 8.2 percent in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan consumer sentiment index, due at 1445 GMT, is expected to fall to 93.5 from January's 103.8. Earlier this week, the Conference Board's consumer confidence index fell harder than expected.  \par \par \uc2 The Chicago Purchasing Managers' Index, due at 1500 GMT, is expected to fall to 63.5 in February from 65.9 the month before.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN LESS AGGRESSIVE?  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 109.14 yen, off this week's three-month high of 109.83. The dollar hit three-year lows near 105.15 earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Japan sold 3.3420 trillion yen ($30.47 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February to stem the yen's rise against the dollar. A senior MOF official said most of the amount was used in the first half of the month.  \par \par \uc2 The figure compares with a record seven trillion yen Japan spent in January.  \par \par \uc2 "The amount (in February) was a little lower than expected. But with dollar/yen at current levels, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be less inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily," Christensen said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier data showed a strong rise in Japan's industrial output in January, underpinning expectations the world's second biggest economy is recovering.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040227e02r001jd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
