\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Market still correcting from strong yen-Japan MOF.  \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2004\par 11:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 27 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is still in the process of correcting excessive yen strength, a senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "I believe we are still in a process of correction. I don't believe it's ended," Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's International Bureau, told reporters. The dollar was hovering around 109 yen on Friday. It rose to 109.84 yen on Thursday, its highest since December 2 and up four percent from three-year lows hit earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said Japanese authorities would continue to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as appropriate to smooth volatile movements.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo sold a record 20 trillion yen ($182.6 billion) last year to curb the yen's rise, followed by a monthly record of over seven trillion yen in January.  \par \par \uc2 MOF data on Friday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 3.3420 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February.  \par \par \uc2 "We will respond when it is necessary," Watanabe said. "If movements are not volatile or disorderly, we don't have to do anything. But there have been such movements, stemming especially from things besides economic factors."  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said dollar sentiment had been weak despite a strong U.S. economy, and such sentiment was exacerbated after a Group of Seven statement issued after a meeting in Dubai last September.  \par \par \uc2 Markets interpreted the statement as a green light to sell the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe added that the statement after the G7 met in Boca Raton in early February had helped change such sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 On a closed-door session with counterparts from China and South Korea held last week, Watanabe said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , macroeconomic issues and structural adjustments were discussed. He declined to elaborate further.  \par \par \uc2 It was a regular meeting held twice a year.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040227e02r0015l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
