\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Japan's Spring Of Dollars May Not Run Eternal  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2004\par 19:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If there was any doubt that Japan's government had plenty of dollars to spend on U.S. government debt securities, data released Friday confirmed it is flush with the green stuff.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 That doesn't necessarily mean the spring of dollars runs eternal though. If the dollar moves another yen higher, this flow of funds that typically finds its way into U.S. debt assets could quickly dry up, say analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Data from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the government sold Y3.342 trillion, or roughly $31.5 billion, in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during February, bringing the year-to-date total to Y10.497 trillion, or more than half that spent in all of last year.  \par \par \uc2 The data don't show which \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Japanese monetary authorities bought, but quarterly breakdowns for 2003 show virtually all \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  targeted dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Bond market participants believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took those dollars and pumped the bulk of them into U.S. Treasurys, something it's been prone to do since it started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets when the dollar first began to buckle over two years ago. In fact, many believe Japan was behind the strong indirect bid in the sale of $56 billion in new Treasury issuance this month. These bidders, made up largely of foreign central banks, bought nearly 50% of the total issuance sold as part of the U.S. government's quarterly refunding.  \par \par \uc2 February's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spending represents less than half the record $67 billion spent in January, but analysts agree it's still enough to keep the Treasury market from slipping on strong U.S. economic data and deep bearish sentiment among domestic investors.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Gerald Lucas, head of U.S Treasury and agency strategy at Banc of America Securities in New York puts it this way:    "$30 billion of buying is still $30 billion of buying. It's still a lot" of money.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But given the dollar's recent strength against its rivals including the yen, its unclear whether the Japanese authorities will need to meddle in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to ensure their export-led recovery stays on track.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're in a murky zone right now about how (the dollar strength) will effect the Treasurys market," said Michael Cheah, portfolio manager at AIG SunAmerica Asset Management, who manages $2.0 billion in fixed income assets. If the dollar continues to move higher without the help from monetary officials, "that's not good for Treasurys," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In just over a week, the dollar has bounced back from Y105.54 to Y109.23 on factors based more on investor positions than anything having to do with economic fundamentals that typically determine a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value. Although it has lost some ground Friday to its European rivals, the dollar was still holding above Y109 against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        A Guiding Hand \par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants believe Japanese authorities have spiked the momentum in the dollar to maximize the move higher, but such a helping hand is likely to disappear if the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves higher of its own accord.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of that flow will dry up if dollar/yen trades above Y110," said Alan Ruskin, director of research at financial consultancy 4Cast in New York, who believes U.S. officials may begin to grumble if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the dollar clears that hurdle. This would be five yen above the Y105 support level that Japanese exporters consider critical to their competitiveness in foreign markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Bank of Japan and other central banks have been no strangers to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets over the past few years, the sharp drop in the dollar beginning last December accelerated their activity with the Bank of Japan showing the most resolve in capping the yen's climb.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They've shown deeper pockets than ever imagined" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, said Ruskin. He estimates the dollar's decline against the yen has only run about half its course, whereas it's largely run its course against the euro, where market forces rather than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation drove the decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With deflation still dogging the economy, low yields and little political fallout from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , many market professionals believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  essentially has been given a blank check to keep its export markets competitive through a weak yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    And The Rest Of Asia? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whether other Asian central banks have similar leeway is unclear. Japan is the largest foreign investor in U.S. Treasurys, counting $545 billion in its portfolio at the end of 2003, up from $385 billion at the beginning of the year. But, other Asian nations make the top ten list of major foreign holders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mainland China comes in at number two with $149 billion, while Hong Kong is ranked fifth with $57.5 billion and South Korea, ninth with $43.4 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anecdotal reports and market speculation suggest these banks are considering a diversification of their assets out of dollar-denominated securities into those based in other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Theoretically this would mean less investment in U.S. Treasurys and other U.S. government debt. But the globalization of economies simply means other investors, not least Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , would shoulder the responsibility of keeping the dollar afloat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To the extent that (other Asian central banks) diversify out of dollars into yen, it just puts that much more pressure on the Bank of Japan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to soak up the dollars sold by these central banks, said Lucas of Banc of America Securities. That is if it wants to keep the yen from strengthening.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    (Jonathan Soble in Tokyo contributed to this article.) \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected February 27, 2004 15:24 ET (20:24 GMT) [ 27-02-04 1840GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They've shown deeper pockets than ever imagined" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, said Ruskin. He estimates the dollar's decline against the yen has only run about half its course, whereas it's largely run its course against the euro, where market forces rather than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation drove the decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (In an item that ran around 1:40 p.m. EST (1840 GMT), Ruskin's outlook for the dollar against the yen was misstated). [ 27-02-04 2021GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040227e02r000xs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
