\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up on concern ECB may cut interest rates.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 18:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar gained half a percent against the euro on Thursday, drawing support from heightened concerns that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut interest rates next week.  \par \par \uc2 Comments from German and French leaders urging the ECB to cut interest rates to protect the fragile export sector boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 3 cents against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 A rate cut would remove some of the euro's appeal, making yields on U.S. assets more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 "The level of uncertainty regarding potential ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ECB rate cuts has increased, and that has contributed to the euro's slide," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst at Ruesch International.  \par \par \uc2 "Also, the increased jawboning by top-level European officials has certainly spooked the market and has prompted the euro to retreat," he added.  \par \par \uc2 In midday New York trade, the euro was down 0.4 percent against the dollar at $1.2445 after falling to a four-week low of $1.2385 after the release of U.S. durable goods data for January.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar traded up 0.4 percent on the yen at 109.51 yen  after\par hitting 109.83, its highest since early December. The dollar rose 0.4\par percent against the Swiss franc to 1.2652 francs       , while sterling\par fell 0.3 percent to $1.8642       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose early in the day after January U.S. durable goods data suggested strength in the economy despite a disappointing headline figure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It has been a pretty choppy trading session, but the dollar has done a good job of not only sustaining yesterday's impressive rally, but even adding to it," said Beuzelin.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  climbed 1.5 percent against the euro on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DURABLE GOODS DATA, EUROPEAN COMMENTS  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar strengthened on news that U.S. non-defense capital goods orders excluding aircraft rose 3.6 percent last month after a 3.8 percent gain in December.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact of an overall 1.8 percent decline in durable goods orders was lessened by a sharp upward revision for December to a 1.6 percent gain, giving investors another reason to buy the dollar. The dollar appeared little affected by new weekly U.S. jobless claims, which rose modestly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market wanted to see a dollar positive here, and they were held back for a couple of seconds by the headline of durable goods. But once they looked beyond it they saw what they wanted," said Steven Englander, chief North American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is still looking to buy dollars today. You are still getting some position-squaring," Englander said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also grew wary of buying euros after German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder on Wednesday urged the ECB to cut rates to help exporters and following a report that the ECB and the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the euro topped $1.35.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Schroeder is on a two-day visit to the United States.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many in the market were skeptical any ECB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was imminent, but traders said a perception was growing that the dollar might have turned the corner after a two-year bear run.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said on Thursday he shared Schroeder's view on European interest rates and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates were not in line with economic reality.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ECB and Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on the joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the ECB faces pressure to cut its official interest rate of 2.0 percent, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy, expressed on Wednesday, stoked speculation that a U.S. rate hike from just 1.0 percent may come sooner, rather than later.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Yesterday's upbeat economic assessment by Federal Reserve Chairman Greenspan has moved forward the timeline for a eventual Fed rate hike," Beuzelin said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040227e02q000j8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
