\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rises after U.S. durable goods data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 16:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday after January U.S. durable goods data suggested strength in the economy despite a disappointing headline figure.  \par \par \uc2 The report gave added upward impetus to the dollar following comments from German and French leaders urging the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates to protect the fragile export sector. A rate cut would remove some of the euro's appeal, making yields on U.S. assets more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 "The market wanted to see a dollar positive here, and they were held back for a couple of seconds by the headline of durable goods. But once they looked beyond it they saw what they wanted," said Steven Englander, chief North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is still looking to buy dollars today. You are still getting some position squaring," Englander said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened on news that U.S. non-defense capital goods orders excluding aircraft rose 3.6 percent last month after a 3.8 percent gain in December.  \par \par \uc2 The impact of an overall 1.8 percent decline in durable goods orders was lessened by a sharp upward revision for December to a 1.6 percent gain, giving investors another reason to buy back the dollar. The dollar appeared little affected by weekly U.S. jobless claims, which rose modestly.  \par \par \uc2 In morning New York trade, the euro was trading down 0.5 percent against the dollar at $1.2429 after falling to a four-week low of $1.2385 after the durables data release.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded up 0.4 percent on the yen at 109.51 yen after hitting 109.83, its highest since early December.  \par \par \uc2 "Durable goods this month is much worse than expected, but because of the revision last month it kind of evens out," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Jobless claims coming in higher than expected illustrates the continued softness in the U.S. labor market. The initial knee-jerk reaction (of the euro) was a spike up and then there was profit taking," she added.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPEANS PUT PRESSURE  \par \par \uc2 Investors grew wary of buying euros after German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder on Wednesday urged the ECB to cut rates to help exporters and following a report that the ECB and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the euro topped $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 Many in the market were sceptical any ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent but traders said a perception was growing that the dollar might have turned the corner after a two-year bear run.  \par \par \uc2 French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said on Thursday he shared Schroeder's view on European interest rates, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were not in line with economic reality.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB and Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined comment on the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  report.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi's boss, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, reiterated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to curb a sharp rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the comments were being used by the market as an excuse to take profits on long euro positions.  \par \par \uc2 While the ECB faces pressure to cut its official interest rate of 2.0 percent, Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy expressed on Wednesday stoked speculation that a U.S. rate hike from just 1.0 percent may come sooner than later.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been sold heavily for months on the prospect of persistently low rates and concerns about the U.S. fiscal and current account deficits. (Additional reporting by John Parry).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q002kj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
