\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends gains, market eyes euro-US rate gap.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to a three-week high on the euro on Thursday, extending recent gains as traders gave more credence to growing expectations of its recovery and took note of euro zone concerns on euro strength.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar also hit near-three-month highs versus the yen after getting a lift on Wednesday from speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or consider easing monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2  Many in the market were sceptical such actions by the ECB were imminent, but traders said perception was growing that the dollar might have turned the corner after a two-year bear run.   \par \par \uc2  "The main thing the market is watching now is the rate differential between the U.S. and the euro zone. If this were to come in this would be bad for the euro and good for the dollar - that's the reason why the euro is having a harder time," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.   \par \par \uc2  "But it's way too soon to be serious about an ECB rate cut. The ECB has shown in the past it's not too aggressive."   \par \par \uc2  The euro was 0.4 percent down from late New York levels, at $1.2440 by 0900 GMT, nearly five cents below this month's record high.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose half a percent to 109.57 yen, its best performance since early December.   \par \par \uc2  ECB IN FOCUS   \par \par \uc2  Investors grew wary of buying euros following comments by German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder on Wednesday pressuring the ECB to cut rates to help Europe's exporters and in the wake of a media report that the ECB and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the euro's rise if it topped $1.35.   \par \par \uc2  French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin said on Thursday he shared Schroeder's view on European interest rates, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were not in line with economic reality.   \par \par \uc2  But analysts were sceptical about whether the ECB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly with the BOJ.   \par \par \uc2  "The ECB is concerned about sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, rather than the euro level. And the United States, on its part, will be unhappy to see them buying dollars," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2  The ECB, as well as Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on the report that the central banks could take joint action.   \par \par \uc2  "We are always in touch with our counterparts, but as for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I won't comment," said Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs.   \par \par \uc2  Still, Mizoguchi's boss, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, reiterated Japan's resolve to curb a sharp rise in the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the authorities would take necessary action in the market as needed.   \par \par \uc2  Against such a background, the yen failed to capitalise on stronger-than-expected Japanese retail sales data or the Nikkei stock price average's 1.47 percent rally.   \par \par \uc2  Retail sales rose for the first time in three months in January, growing 1.3 percent from the same month a year earlier on strong demand for cars and a rebound in clothing sales.   \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's decisions to keep monetary policy unchanged, as expected, also had no visible impact on the yen.   \par \par \uc2  FED RATE HIKE   \par \par \uc2  While the ECB faced pressure to cut its official interest rate of 2.0 percent, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy on Wednesday stoked hope that a U.S. rate hike may come sooner than later.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar had been sold heavily across the board for months due to prospects of persistently low U.S. interest rates and concerns about the U.S. fiscal and current account deficits.   \par \par \uc2  The Fed is widely seen to have refrained from increasing rates because strong U.S. growth in the second half of 2003 has yet to create enough new jobs.   \par \par \uc2  For more hints about the dollar's direction, investors will be looking at U.S. initial jobless claims data due at 1330 GMT and January durable goods orders data, also at 1330 GMT. Fed board governors Susan Schmidt Bies and Ben Bernanke will speak at 1800 GMT and 2200 GMT respectively.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q001gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
