\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steady, hovers near recent peaks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 08:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar was largely steady on Thursday as its gains the previous day gave more credence to growing optimism about its recovery, but caution set in after the advance stalled slightly short of recent peaks.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar retained most its gains from Wednesday, when it rose toward Monday's three-week highs against the euro and three-month highs versus the yen on speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or ease monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Many in the market were sceptical such actions by the ECB were imminent, but traders said perception was growing that the dollar might have turned the corner after a two-year bear run.  \par \par \uc2 "It's increasingly looking like the down moves are the counter-trend corrections and the up moves are the main trend," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the corporate desk at Trust & Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \uc2 He noted that the dollar, after gaining nearly four percent from last week's record lows against the euro, only gave back half of that ground before bouncing back up again on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at $1.2489/94 to the euro at 0614 GMT, virtually unchanged on the day and within a half cent of three-week highs around $1.2450. The dollar was also flat at 108.77/82 yen within sight of Monday's three-month highs around 109.40 yen.  \par \par \uc2 ECB IN FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 Investors grew wary of buying euros following comments by German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder on Wednesday pressuring the ECB to cut rates to help Europe's exporters.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, Market News International cited unnamed sources as saying that the ECB and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the euro's rise if it topped $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts were sceptical about whether the ECB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly with the BOJ.  \par \par \uc2 "The ECB is concerned about sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, rather than the euro level. And the United States, on its part, will be unhappy to see them buying dollars," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB, as well as Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on the report that the central banks could take joint action.  \par \par \uc2 "We are always in touch with our counterparts, but as for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I won't comment," said Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2 Still, Mizoguchi's boss, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, reiterated Japan's resolve to curb a sharp rise in the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying the authorities would take necessary action in the market as needed.  \par \par \uc2 Against such a background, the yen failed to capitalise on stronger-than-expected Japanese retail sales data or the Nikkei stock price average's 1.47 percent rally.  \par \par \uc2 Retail sales rose for the first time in three months in January, growing 1.3 percent from the same month a year earlier on strong demand for cars and a rebound in clothing sales.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ's decisions to keep monetary policy unchanged, as expected, also had no visible impact on the yen.  \par \par \uc2 FED RATE HIKE  \par \par \uc2 While the ECB faced pressure to cut its official interest rate of 2.0 percent, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy on Wednesday stoked hope that a U.S. rate hike may come sooner than later.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had been sold heavily across the board for months due to prospects of persistently low U.S. interest rates and concerns about the U.S. fiscal and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely seen to have refrained from increasing rates because strong U.S. growth in the second half of 2003 has yet to create enough new jobs.  \par \par \uc2 For more hints about the dollar's direction, investors will be looking at U.S. initial jobless claims data due at 1330 GMT and durable goods orders data, also at 1330 GMT. Fed board governors Susan Schmidt and Ben Bernanke will speak at 1800 GMT and 2200 GMT respectively.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q0013s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
