\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-BOJ keeps policy steady, may smooth JGB trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando  \par \uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 08:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan kept policy unchanged on Thursday, taking heart from the fastest economic growth in 13 years, but showed it is still prepared to act by saying it may tweak rules to improve government bond market liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ Policy Board decided by a unanimous vote to keep its target volume for current account deposits parked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by commercial banks at 30-35 trillion yen ($275-321 billion), as expected by financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 That came as little surprise after data last week showed 1.7 percent economic growth in October-December over the previous quarter, helped by robust exports and capital spending.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ has repeatedly raised the target over the past year in an effort to encourage business activity and stamp out deflation, which is seen crimping corporate profits and discouraging consumption.  \par \par \uc2 Current account deposits are bank reserves and reserves of other financial institutions such as brokerages, and the target is a benchmark for the banking system's liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The target was last raised in January from 27-32 trillion yen. That move surprised financial markets as the economy had been showing signs of a firm, export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But in a sign it has not abandoned its aggressive policy stance, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it may adopt changes to help boost liquidity in the Japanese government bond (JGB) market.  \par \par \uc2 PROACTIVE STANCE  \par \par \uc2 It said it will consider offering JGBs it holds to the market as a secondary source, a facility known as securities lending, to help ensure availability of issues with low liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 "In government securities markets, liquidity may decline and pricing may be hampered occasionally when market participants experience difficulties in securing specific issues or face uncertainties over their availability," it said.  \par \par \uc2 "In such cases, market participants should strive to maintain market liquidity and to avert a negative impact on the markets."  \par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui later told a news conference: "This is one new step in our long-term effort to improve the JGB market."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said liquidity was relatively low in 15-, 20-and 30-year JGBs as well as some futures contracts before roll-overs.  \par \par \uc2 Izuru Kato, chief economist at Totan Research, said the move may have a positive impact on markets, though details are not yet known.  \par \par \uc2 "It's big news for the repo and JGB markets, but we will need to see what kind of restrictions and criteria they set for the lending facility," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Many agreed, saying it could help modify the yield curve and prevent sudden spikes.  \par \par \uc2 Some said it was not standard monetary policy and therefore would mean little for the overall economy, but they too said it may have a psychological effect by showing the BOJ and government were working more closely than ever.  \par \par \uc2 "This announcement shows that the BOJ is keen to show it is working with the government to soothe concerns about Japan's fiscal debts...and to relieve any stress in the market," said Naoki Murakami, a senior economist at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 The bond market, however, showed little initial reaction.  \par \par \uc2 Key 10-year JGB futures for March delivery were down 0.10 point on the day at 140.25. They were slightly below 140.30 at midday before the BOJ's announcement.  \par \par \uc2 RISKIER STEPS  \par \par \uc2 The move follows a series of other technical changes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has had to turn to increasingly creative steps in recent years with interest rates already near zero.  \par \par \uc2 It has said it will accept inflation-linked JGBs as collateral for market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from March, in addition to JGBs, foreign government bonds, short-term bills, corporate bonds and asset-backed securities with low credit risks.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, some analysts say it may turn to even riskier policies such as buying shares from the market if the yen surges or the stock market plunges.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ now buys shares from banks as a temporary measure to protect the frail industry from market fluctuations.  \par \par \uc2 But further risk-taking could end up damaging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet, which could prove disastrous for Japan's external credibility since the government's tactics on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also under question.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 20 trillion yen in 2003 and another seven trillion in January to rein in the yen's value, meaning the government has acquired a massive pile of government debt and foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q000hn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
