\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steady on joint  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 , rate concerns.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara   \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2004\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 26 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied on Thursday as investors considered the possibility of euro-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB), which also came under pressure to cut interest rates to stem the euro's rise.   \par \par \uc2 Investors grew cautious about chasing up the euro against the dollar after Market News International cited unnamed sources as saying that the ECB and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the euro's rise if it topped $1.35.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar held onto most of Wednesday's gains to trade around $1.2500 at 0247 GMT, up more than three percent from life lows hit last week, but down half a cent from Monday's high of $1.2450.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts were sceptical about whether the ECB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly with the BOJ.   \par \par \uc2 "The ECB is concerned about sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, rather than the euro level and the United States, on its part, will be unhappy to see them buying dollars," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. welcomes gradual falls in the dollar, as seen in recent remarks from Fed officials addressing negative views about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with (Richmond Fed President Alfred) Broaddus saying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hurt the Fed's credibility."   \par \par \uc2 The ECB, as well as Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on the report that the central banks could take joint action.   \par \par \uc2 "We are always in touch with our counterparts, but as for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I won't comment," Mizoguchi said.   \par \par \uc2 Remarks by Bundesbank Vice President Juergen Stark reinforced such doubts.   \par \par \uc2 Stark said on Thursday in New York that the financing of the giant U.S. current account deficit cannot go on forever if foreign central banks start rethinking their buying of dollar assets.   \par \par \uc2 As a large portion of dollars raised by Asian central banks through \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thought to have been poured into U.S. Treasuries, analysts took Stark's comment as a criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "To me, Stark's words suggest that Europe doesn't want to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its own cost," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.   \par \par \uc2 Stark also said the global upswing has more impact on euro zone exports than a strong euro.   \par \par \uc2 That remark was taken by the market as a reply to coments from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder on Wednesday pressuring the ECB to cut rates to help Europe's exporters.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was fetching around 109.00 yen not far from Monday's three-month high of 109.40 yen, and up one percent from 108.01 lows hit this week.   \par \par \uc2 The yen failed to climb on stronger than expected retail sales data. Retail sales rose for the first time in three percent of 1.3 percent from January 2003 helped by strong demand for cars and a rebound in clothing sales.   \par \par \uc2 The yen was also unfazed by the BOJ's decision to keep monetary policy unchanged as expected at its policy meeting on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 US OUTLOOK BRIGHT?   \par \par \uc2 While the ECB faced pressure to cut its official interest rate of 2.0 percent, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's positive outlook on the U.S. economy stoked hope that a U.S. rate hike may come sooner than later.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been sold heavily across the board for months due to prospects of persistently low U.S. interest rates and concerns about the U.S. fiscal and current account deficits.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed is refraining from increasing rates because strong U.S. growth in the second half of 2003 has yet to create new jobs.   \par \par \uc2 Cleveland Fed President Sandra Pianalto said on Thursday during Asian time that strong U.S. productivity and job outsourcing helped explain weak U.S. jobs creation, but conditions were getting better.   \par \par \uc2 For more hints about the dollar's direction, investors will be looking at U.S. initial jobless claims data due at 1330 GMT and durable goods orders data also at 1330 GMT. Fed Board Governor Susan Schmidt and Ben Bernanke will speak at 1800 GMT and 2200 GMT respectively.   \par \par \uc2 In Europe, German consumer inflation data is due at 0700 GMT and the French business confidence survey at 0745 GMT.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040226e02q000f4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
